# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-17 round 233. The two counts I promised Igor, and the other half of
# the table.
#
# WHAT ROUND 232 SETTLED. Forcing SIZE_E_2 to 1 everywhere times out at 16
# output channels and gives 0 of 41 at 41, while the predicate gives 16 of 16
# and 41 of 41. Each form fails where the other works, so the toggle is real on
# RK3576 and it selects SIZE_E_2, which is Igor's reading rather than my fit.
#
# WHAT IGOR THEN MEASURED, and it is the opposite of what this board does. He
# built conv2d-cal's regime on RK3588, out_zp 128 and no fused activation, and
# compared against both references:
#
#   oc  56    56 of 56 against the RAW cpu, 0 of 56 against max(cpu, zp)
#   oc 128   128 of 128 against the RAW cpu, 6 of 128 against max(cpu, zp)
#
# Where the raw reference falls below the zero point his hardware output spans
# 0 to 127 rather than sitting at 128, and only 0.6 to 0.8 percent of values
# land exactly on the zero point, which is chance. He ruled out a silent CPU
# fallback three ways: the per core interrupt counters advance, 15 of 128
# channels differ from the CPU by one where a fallback would be bit identical,
# and half the raw reference lies below the zero point so there was something
# for a clamp to remove.
#
# SO RK3588 DOES NOT CLAMP AT THE OUTPUT ZERO POINT. That is one more thing the
# clamp here is not, and the expensive one to have ruled out by reading.
#
# HIS QUESTION, WHICH THIS ROUND DOES NOT ANSWER. He cannot tell whether the
# difference is the silicon or the userspace, since his runs are upstream Mesa
# and mine are this tree. His suggestion, stock upstream Mesa on RK3576, is not
# runnable: upstream has no RK3576 support, so it would drive this hardware
# through the RK3588 encoder, which round 224 already showed is a page fault
# storm. The clean version of his experiment is the VENDOR userspace on this
# silicon, and that image is built and waiting: geom/a_lin is a linear
# convolution the vendor quantised to out_zp 145 with 58.6 percent of its float
# output negative. It needs the other card and the vendor SPI U-Boot.
#
# THIS ROUND IS THE SWEEP I OWED HIM. 88 and 120 output channels, which are
# exactly the counts he already has on RK3588, so both forms end up measured on
# both SoCs at four counts.
#
#   the predicate gives SIZE_E_2 1 at both, since DIV_ROUND_UP(88,16) and
#   DIV_ROUND_UP(120,16) are 6 and 8, both even
#   the modulo form gives 0 at both, since 88 and 120 are 24 mod 32
#
# so ROCKET_DPU4050_MOD32 is the discriminating knob here, not the constant.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   88 and 120 correct under the predicate and broken under the modulo form
#             the rule holds at four counts on this SoC and the table is
#             symmetric with his. SIZE_E_2 is the field and parity is how it is
#             chosen here.
#   both correct under both
#             those counts do not discriminate either, and the rule rests on 56
#             alone plus the two small counts, which is thinner than it looks.
#   the predicate is wrong at 88 or 120
#             parity is not the rule and the three points it was fitted on were
#             a coincidence.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-17 round 232. Igor decoded the two constants and the difference is one
# bit, so ask whether the predicate is needed at all.
#
# WHAT HE FOUND, on the v7 thread. 0x80011111 and 0x80011011 differ ONLY in
# bit 8, which is the low bit of SIZE_E_2 at bits 8 to 10 in DPU_BS_OW_CFG. The
# form that works at 56 output channels has SIZE_E_2 = 1 and the one that times
# out has 0, and upstream Mesa emits a constant for every count whose SIZE_E_2
# is 1. He also points out that these values carry RGP_CNTER = 8, OW_SRC = 1 and
# two bits in the reserved range that upstream never sets.
#
# WHAT HE MEASURED, on RK3588 with upstream Mesa and its constant, against an
# UNCLAMPED per channel CPU reference, three jobs landing one per core:
#
#   1x1 conv, 64 in, 56 out    56 of 56 within one
#   1x1 conv, 64 in, 88 out    88 of 88 within one
#   1x1 conv, 64 in, 120 out  120 of 120 within one
#
# So RK3588 needs no output channel dependent toggle there. That is the third
# possibility my two fitted forms could not tell apart, and neither of them
# proposed it.
#
# WHAT THIS BOARD HAS ACTUALLY SHOWN, which is a smaller claim than I have been
# making. Every measurement is of the value my predicate happens to emit:
#
#   oc  16   emits SIZE_E_2 = 0   correct
#   oc  41   emits SIZE_E_2 = 0   correct
#   oc  56   emits SIZE_E_2 = 1   correct, and 0 reproduces the timeout
#   oc  64   emits SIZE_E_2 = 1   correct
#   oc 128   emits SIZE_E_2 = 1   correct
#
# The only cell never filled in is SIZE_E_2 = 1 at 16 and at 41. If those work
# too, RK3576 needs no toggle either, the parity rule I fitted is complexity
# with nothing behind it, and it comes out of the upstream patch before the
# series is posted. ROCKET_DPU4050_CONST forces the constant, so this costs one
# boot on the working driver.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   every shape correct with the constant
#             the predicate is unnecessary on RK3576 as well, and the upstream
#             encoder emits one value like upstream's own does. A fitted rule
#             removed is worth more than a fitted rule defended.
#   16 or 41 breaks under the constant
#             the toggle is real on RK3576 and the parity form stays, now with
#             a reason rather than a fit: SIZE_E_2 is what it selects.
#   56 breaks under the constant
#             that would contradict round 220's reach control, and the run is
#             suspect before anything else is read.
#
# ⚠ THIS ROUND USES THE WORKING DRIVER, because ROCKET_DPU4050_CONST is one of
# the 118 knobs and exists only there. The clean series is still in the image
# and round 231's questions are still unanswered; the card had round 229 on it
# when that log was taken.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 231. Two questions, two drivers, one image.
#
# QUESTION ONE, unchanged from round 230. Does the series rebased onto upstream
# main behave the same as the one round 229 verified against 25.3.0. One patch
# was dropped, because upstream deleted calc_explicit_padding, and the rest
# needed API adaptation.
#
# QUESTION TWO, which has never actually been answered. Does this thing
# classify.
#
# ⚠ WHAT HAS BEEN MEASURED IS NOT THAT. Every MobileNet number in this log book
# is CHANNEL AGREEMENT: 1000 of 1001 output channels within one count. That says
# the two surfaces agree. It does not say the two stacks name the same class,
# and on a classifier that is the only thing anyone wants. The output is a
# quantised softmax with about ten distinct values, so two classes can sit one
# count apart and a single count is enough to swap them. perch.py now prints the
# top five and whether the argmax agrees, which the whole project has inferred
# for months and never once printed.
#
# ⚠ AND IT HAS TO RUN ON THE WORKING DRIVER TO MEAN ANYTHING. The clean series
# declines depthwise, pointwise and the first convolution, so MobileNet falls
# back to the CPU under it and the comparison would be the CPU against itself,
# where the argmax agrees by construction and proves nothing. So this image
# carries BOTH libraries and the MobileNet entry uses the 102 commit one.
#
#   /usr/lib/libteflon.so           five patches on upstream main
#   /usr/lib/libteflon-working.so   the 102 commit driver
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   entries 1 to 4 match round 229 number for number
#             the rebase onto main changed no behaviour and the series can go
#             to the fork.
#   entry 5 says TOP 1 AGREES on the working driver
#             the NPU classifies the same image the same way the CPU does, and
#             the answer to the question is yes with a measurement behind it.
#   entry 5 says TOP 1 DIFFERS
#             then 1000 of 1001 channels within one has been hiding a label
#             flip all along, which would be the most important thing this log
#             book has said in a while.
#   entry 6, the same model on the clean series, must say the top five agree
#             trivially. It is there to show what a vacuous comparison looks
#             like next to a real one.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 230. The same series, rebased onto upstream main.
#
# ROUND 229 PASSED EVERYTHING, on a series built against Mesa 25.3.0.
#
#   conv2d-cal   128 of 128, clamp footprint 100820 of 204800
#   cal_k3       128 of 128, footprint 98391
#   cal_oc16      16 of 16,  footprint 12566
#   mn_conv0      32 of 32   declined, ran on the CPU, correct
#   mobilenet   1001 of 1001 declined, ran on the CPU, correct
#   conv2d-cal   128 of 128 again, no timeouts, no page faults
#
# WHAT IS DIFFERENT HERE. gitlab.freedesktop.org came back, so the series is now
# five patches on top of main rather than six on top of 25.3.0.
#
#   one patch was DROPPED. Upstream deleted calc_explicit_padding entirely and
#   takes the padding from the operation now, so the axis swap it fixed has no
#   code left to fix.
#
#   the API moved. pipe_ml_subgraph no longer carries a context, there is a
#   pipe_ml_device and rkt_ml_operation_supported takes it, rkt_fill_biases no
#   longer maps its own tensors, and ALIGN became align. The rocket directory
#   has drifted about 380 lines from 25.3.0, and upstream has still not touched
#   RK3576 at all.
#
# ⚠ NONE OF THAT HAS RUN ON THIS BOARD. The adaptation is mechanical, and
# mechanical changes to code whose behaviour I had not measured are exactly what
# went wrong five times between round 223 and round 228. So it gets measured
# rather than assumed, with the same models and the same rule as round 229.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   every number matches round 229's, footprints included
#             the series is verified on hardware against upstream main and can
#             go to the fork as it is.
#   any number moves
#             the adaptation changed behaviour somewhere, and the model that
#             moved says where. The 25.3.0 branch is still here to diff
#             against, function by function.
#   a decline turns into a wrong answer
#             rkt_ml_operation_supported reaches the screen through the new ml
#             device now, and that is the one adapted line whose failure would
#             be silent rather than loud.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 229. The clean branch computes. Now run the regression on it.
#
# ROUND 228, THREE RUNS, ALL THE SAME.
#
#   MESA: info: rocket: RK3576
#   rocket: coef oc=128 table=1024 scales=256 op2_off=1280 total=206080
#   rocket: A[0..3] -54 3221 10107 -1021   B[0] -5   C[0] 16
#   rocket: scale[0]=43d3   op2=00001004
#   conv2d-cal 128 of 128, clamp footprint 100820 of 204800
#
# Every field checks against something known independently. The table is 16
# records of 64 bytes, the scales are 128 entries of 2, the operand offset 1280
# is the same number the encoder puts in 0x5024, the total is the working
# driver's allocation to the byte, B is 0x80 minus the model's weight zero point
# of 133, and C is 16 because the tensor is per tensor quantised. And the answer
# is the working driver's own, footprint included.
#
# So 653 lines reproduce what 3979 did, for the shape the series claims.
#
# FIVE FAILURES GOT HERE AND ALL FIVE WERE MINE. The buffer size, the missing
# operand word, the detection default, and then two wrong explanations of where
# the OF node lives. Every one the same shape: a change made to a patch because
# it read better, to code whose behaviour I had not measured. Three audits
# caught two of them, and only because they compared against something known to
# work. The last one ended when the board printed a map instead of me proposing
# a fifth depth.
#
# THIS ROUND. The temporary coefficient print is gone from the series, so what
# runs here is exactly what would be posted. The question is whether the other
# two shapes in the same regime work, and whether the shapes the series declines
# really do fall back to the CPU rather than being encoded wrongly.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   cal_k3 and cal_oc16 both come back all channels correct
#             the series covers its regime rather than one model in it. It is
#             ready to rebase onto upstream and go out.
#   either is wrong
#             the encoder is fitted to conv2d-cal, and the difference between
#             them names what: cal_k3 varies the kernel, cal_oc16 the output
#             channel count.
#   mn_conv0 or MobileNet come back WRONG rather than correct and slow
#             the guard does not work and the series would silently mis-encode
#             a first convolution, which is worse than not supporting it.
#
# mn_conv0 is 224x224x3 and MobileNet is mostly depthwise and pointwise, so both
# should now be declined and run on the CPU. Correct and slow is the pass here.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 228. The map says the OF node is not on that branch at all.
#
# WHAT ROUND 227 PRINTED, and it settles four rounds of wrong guesses.
#
#   /sys/dev/char/261:0 -> /sys/devices/platform/rknn/accel/accel0
#   no of_node at accel0, at accel, at rknn, at platform
#   /sys/bus/platform/devices/27700000.npu/of_node/compatible
#       = rockchip,rk3576-rknn-core
#
# The accel node's parent is the synthetic platform device the driver creates
# to aggregate the cores. The device that carries the OF node is 27700000.npu,
# a SIBLING of it on the platform bus, not an ancestor. No amount of climbing
# was ever going to reach it, which is why one depth, then three, then a full
# walk all failed. Climbing was the wrong instrument and the rule written before
# round 227 said that was the second thing it could turn out to be.
#
# WHAT CHANGED. The probe scans the platform bus for a device whose compatible
# names an rknn core and reads the SoC out of it. There is one NPU per SoC so
# the first core is the answer. It still refuses to open rather than guessing.
#
# This wants to be a parameter on the uAPI instead of sysfs archaeology, and
# saying so belongs in the series when it goes out.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   rocket: RK3576 prints, then the three coef lines, then a result
#             the clean branch runs as itself for the first time. Read the
#             result: 128 of 128 with a 100820 pixel clamp footprint is the
#             working driver's own answer.
#   rocket: RK3576 prints and the result is empty or faults
#             the SoC is finally right and something else is wrong. The coef
#             lines say what was in the buffer, which is the question round 225
#             was built to ask and has never yet been able to.
#   it still cannot tell
#             the scan found no rknn core on the platform bus, which contradicts
#             what round 227 printed, and that would be worth understanding
#             before anything else.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 227. The refusal worked. Now find where the OF node is.
#
# ROUND 226 CONFIRMED THE DIAGNOSIS AND KILLED MY EXPLANATION OF IT.
#
#   accel0 is 261,0
#   device/of_node/compatible MISSING
#   device/device/of_node/compatible MISSING
#   device/device/device/of_node/compatible MISSING
#   MESA: error: rocket: cannot tell which NPU this is
#
# So the RK3588 fallback really was what drove round 224's storm, and refusing
# to open rather than guessing did its job: the board came back with a clean
# error instead of 18785 page faults, and teflon ran the model on the CPU.
#
# ⚠ BUT MY REASON FOR THE PROBE FAILING WAS ALSO WRONG. I said the OF node was
# one level further up than the code looked. It is not at one, two or three
# levels up either. That was a fourth guess and the board refused it too.
#
# So this round stops proposing depths. The probe resolves the char device's
# REAL path and climbs it until a compatible turns up, which does not assume
# anything about the shape of the tree, and the script prints that real path
# and every of_node under the device so a failure is a map rather than another
# hypothesis.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   rocket: RK3576 prints
#             the climb found it. Read the coefficient lines and the result,
#             which is the first time this branch will have run as itself.
#   it still cannot tell
#             the printed tree says where the OF node actually is, or that
#             there is not one reachable from the accel node at all. If it is
#             the latter, sysfs is the wrong instrument and the SoC has to come
#             from somewhere else, which is a design question rather than a
#             bug.
#
# Nothing else changed. The encoder, the records and the guard are as they were
# in round 226, and none of them has ever run on this board yet.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 226. The clean branch was not running as an RK3576 at all.
#
# ROUND 225 ANSWERED IT, by printing nothing. The three ROCKET_COEF_DUMP lines
# never appeared, and they sit inside the branch guarded by
# soc == RKT_SOC_RK3576. perch.py ran, a job was submitted, and the answer came
# back empty, so the driver was alive and the RK3576 branch was simply never
# taken. Everything fell to the RK3588 path: its bias array, its encoder, its
# register map, driven at RK3576 hardware. That is the address storm, and it is
# why round 224 looked worse than 223 rather than better.
#
# ⚠ THE CAUSE IS MINE AGAIN, AND IT IS THE THIRD TIME IN THIS SERIES. Reviewing
# the clean patches I changed the SoC detection default from RK3576 to RK3588,
# on the grounds that a failed probe must not turn an RK3588 into an RK3576.
# The direction of that argument is right. What it missed is that the probe was
# ALREADY FAILING on this board: the accel char device's parent is the accel
# device, not the platform device that carries the OF node, so
# /sys/dev/char/M:m/device/of_node/compatible does not exist. The working
# driver's default of RK3576 had been covering that up since the day it was
# written, and nothing ever printed which SoC it thought it had.
#
# THREE GUESSES, THREE WRONG, ALL THREE MINE, and all three the same shape: a
# change made to a patch because it read better, to code whose behaviour I had
# not measured. The buffer size, the operand word region, the detection
# default. The audits caught the first two only because they compared against
# something known to work.
#
# WHAT CHANGED. The probe walks up the device chain instead of assuming a
# depth, and matches either SoC positively. If neither turns up it now REFUSES
# to create the screen rather than picking one, and says so. A screen that will
# not open is a message; a screen that opens on the wrong assumption is 18785
# lines of page faults. And it logs which SoC it decided on, so this can never
# again be invisible.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   "rocket: RK3576" prints, then the three coef lines, then a result
#             the detection was the whole of it. Read the result normally.
#   "rocket: RK3576" prints and the coef lines follow, but the result is
#   still empty or faulting
#             the SoC is right and something else is wrong, and for the first
#             time the log will say which buffer with what in it.
#   the screen refuses to open
#             the walk still does not find the OF node, and the next step is to
#             look at the sysfs tree on the board rather than to guess a depth.
#             Teflon will fall back to the CPU, so perch.py still answers.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 225. Two wrong guesses is enough. Make the board say it.
#
# WHAT ROUND 224 DID. 18785 lines of IOMMU read faults and nothing else. Not one
# line of perch.py output, not even a block header. The faults sweep
#
#   0x300000 0x400000 0x500000 0x600000 0x700000 0x800000 0x900000
#
# in one megabyte steps with offsets of 0x1, 0x11, 0x21 and 0x31, and every one
# reports dte valid: 0, so nothing is mapped anywhere near them. That is a unit
# walking a list of addresses that are not addresses.
#
# It is WORSE than round 223, which faulted once at 0x600001 and then timed out.
# Adding the operand word made the descriptor valid, so the unit now starts a
# fetch it previously never began, and the fetch is reading garbage.
#
# ⚠ I HAVE GUESSED THE CAUSE TWICE AND BEEN WRONG TWICE. First the buffer size,
# then, after an audit found the missing operand word, that. Both were real
# defects and both are fixed, and neither is this. What I have checked since,
# and can rule out:
#
#   the bank constants I changed this round do NOT move conv2d-cal's split. It
#   is one task before and after, and the numbers now match the working driver
#   exactly (entries per slice 10, input banks 2, weight banks 1, 768 slices
#   available against 80 needed).
#
#   the float surface region after the operand word is the same size as the
#   working driver's, 204800 bytes, so the operand cannot be reading off the end
#   of the allocation.
#
#   0x1004 satisfies the constraint the working driver records for that word,
#   (w & 0x3f) == 0x04 and ((w >> 6) & 0xff) >= 0x21.
#
# So I do not know, and this round does not guess. It runs ONE model ONCE and
# prints what the driver actually built, because nothing in round 224's log says
# which buffer was being read or what was in it.
#
# ROCKET_COEF_DUMP is temporary and marked as such in the source. It prints the
# coefficient layout, the first record and the operand word.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   the layout line prints and then conv2d-cal answers
#             read the answer normally. The storm was something later in round
#             224's script, not the first model, and the log simply lost its
#             beginning.
#   the layout line prints and then the faults start
#             the coefficient buffer is built as intended and something reads
#             it wrongly, so the fault is in the encoder's addresses rather
#             than in the buffer. The printed offsets are then directly
#             comparable with 0x5020 and 0x5024.
#   nothing prints at all
#             it faults before the first convolution is even built, which puts
#             it outside everything looked at so far.
#
# ⚠ IF THE BOARD IS UNUSABLE, the working driver is one image away. It is the
# rk3576 branch of the Mesa tree, and round 222's image was the last one built
# from it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 224. The clean branch faulted, and the cause was a change I
# made while reviewing it.
#
# WHAT ROUND 223 DID. conv2d-cal came back 0 of 128 with every pixel pinned at
# out_zp, an empty convolution, and beside it
#
#   rocket 27700000.npu: NPU job timed out
#   rk_iommu: Page fault at 0x0000000000600001 of type read
#
# and by the third attempt the IOMMU could not be reset and the machine took an
# SError. That is the third branch of round 223's rule, a read past the end of a
# buffer, and the branch named the buffer.
#
# ⚠ MY FIRST ATTRIBUTION WAS WRONG, and an audit found the real one. I blamed
# the coefficient buffer, which I had shrunk while reviewing the series on the
# grounds that sizing it by the weight element count had nothing to do with what
# the hardware reads. That was a bad change and it is reverted, but it is not
# what emptied the convolution.
#
# WHAT ACTUALLY BROKE IT. Past the records and the per channel scales sits the
# word the SDP's second operand points at, and 0x5024 points exactly there. The
# working driver writes 0x1004 into it. The clean series wrote nothing, so the
# unit read a zero, which is not a valid descriptor. The working driver's own
# comment records the same configuration from round 46, when the pointer was
# moved past the scale table and nothing was written at the new target, and the
# shapes that had been passing went from 128 of 128 to 0 of 128. The IOMMU read
# fault is downstream of that, not of the allocation.
#
# The clean series now writes both things it was missing: the fp16 per channel
# scale table, and the operand word.
#
# The lesson is narrower than "do not clean up". An allocation whose size I
# cannot explain is not evidence that the size is wrong, and a region I do not
# understand is not evidence that nothing has to be in it. Both of my changes
# there were style reasoning applied to behaviour.
#
# ⚠ ALSO FOUND, AND IT HAS BEEN WRONG FOR MANY ROUNDS. The boot line
#
#   echo 1 > /sys/module/rocket/parameters/task_con16
#
# has been failing every time, because that parameter does not exist. There is
# no module_param anywhere in the rocket driver. The RK3576 task number layout
# is written unconditionally in rocket_job.c, so the fix has always been in
# effect and nothing measured is invalidated, but every round header that said
# "the boot line applies the fix" was describing something that never ran. The
# line is gone from this round.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   conv2d-cal is 128 of 128 with a 100820 pixel clamp footprint
#             the clean branch computes what the working driver does, and the
#             only thing that ever separated them was the allocation.
#   still empty, but no fault
#             the records are wrong rather than the buffer, which the identical
#             register stream had already narrowed it to.
#   still faulting
#             something else in the rewrite reads past a buffer, and the next
#             step is to print the allocation and the addresses rather than
#             guess again.
#
# ⚠ mn_conv0 AND MobileNet SHOULD NOW RUN ON THE CPU, not on the NPU. The clean
# series declines a depthwise convolution, a pointwise one, and one whose input
# has up to four channels, because it implements none of them and was accepting
# them silently. mn_conv0 is 224x224x3 and MobileNet is full of the other two.
# So they are expected to be CORRECT and slow rather than wrong and fast, and a
# wrong answer from either of them now means the guard does not work.
#
# The unsupported shapes are LAST this round rather than in the middle. Round
# 223 put them after the measurement and the device was already gone by then.
#
# ⚠ mn_conv0 MOVED, and round 223 had it in the wrong place. It is 224x224x3,
# so its three input channels put it on the working driver's FIRST CONVOLUTION
# path, both for the weight layout and for the encoder, and this series
# implements neither. Round 223 listed it under "the same regime at other
# sizes", which was a label rather than a fact. It belongs with the shapes that
# are expected to be wrong, and it is there now. What is left in entry 2 really
# is the same regime: cal_k3 and cal_oc16 are both 16 input channels over
# 80x80 per tensor uint8, varying only the kernel and the output channel count.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 223. Does the clean branch actually compute, or only emit
# the same bytes.
#
# WHAT THIS IMAGE CARRIES. Not the working driver. libteflon.so here is built
# from rk3576-clean, four patches on top of Mesa 25.3.0 with no development
# history in them.
#
#   546 lines against 3979, and none of the 118 environment variables. The
#   weight packing, the submit path and the output readback are untouched,
#   because a regular convolution already takes the path that was there.
#
# It was verified offline first. The clean encoder's stream for conv2d-cal was
# reconstructed and compared against the stream round 221 printed from the
# working driver on this board, and it is 143 of 143 entries identical with the
# order included.
#
# ⚠ THAT IS NOT THE SAME AS COMPUTING. An identical register stream still
# leaves the weight buffer and the coefficient records to be right, and those
# are rewritten code rather than copied. The coefficient buffer in particular is
# now sized by what the hardware reads instead of by the weight element count,
# which is a smaller allocation than the working driver made, and nothing has
# ever tested that the smaller one is enough.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   conv2d-cal is 128 of 128 with the same 100820 pixel clamp footprint
#             the clean branch computes what the working one does, and the
#             first upstream series stands on a measurement rather than on a
#             byte comparison.
#   it is 0 of 128 or pinned flat
#             the coefficient records or the buffer size are wrong. The stream
#             is known identical, so the fault is in one of those two and
#             nowhere else, which is a much shorter list than usual.
#   it times out
#             the buffer is too small and the hardware read past it.
#
# The other three shapes are expected to FAIL and are here to say how. They are
# a pointwise convolution, a channel count that is not a multiple of the atom,
# and MobileNet, and this series deliberately does not implement any of them.
# What matters is that they fail cleanly rather than wedging the device, since
# an upstream series that hangs a board on an unsupported shape is worse than
# one that computes nothing.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 222. The register stream is exhausted, and last round's
# whole graph result could not tell running from not running.
#
# WHAT ROUND 221 SETTLED. The control held, conv2d-cal being 128 of 128 with
# the dump on and off and the clamp footprint identical, so the instrument did
# not change the experiment.
#
# THE SAME MODEL DIFF CAME BACK CLEAN. Mesa's assembled stream for conv2d-cal
# against the vendor's compile of the same geometry, geom/g_cal, differs in ten
# registers and every one of them is accounted for.
#
#   five are addresses     CNA 0x1088 and 0x1110, DPU 0x4018, RDMA 0x5020 and
#                          0x5024. The vendor's file carries zero and the
#                          runtime patches them.
#   three are the requant  DPU 0x40ac, 0x40b0, 0x40b4. Different weights, and
#                          Mesa's 0x40ac of 0 is out_zp 128, which is right.
#   one is the pad value   CNA 0x1084, in_zp - 0x80 since June. conv2d-cal is
#                          in_zp 128 so 0, the vendor's model is in_zp 0 so
#                          -128.
#   one is the padding     CNA 0x1080. Mesa 0x02020101 is before 1 after 2,
#                          which is TFLite SAME for 5x5 stride 2. The vendor's
#                          0x01010202 is before 2 after 1, which is what
#                          gen_geom.py asks PyTorch for. Both are right for
#                          their own model.
#
# Plus four OP_EN writes Mesa makes and the vendor does not, which is the known
# per unit engage. Nothing else. So the clamp that loses the negative half of
# every symmetric output is NOT in the register stream, and the coefficient
# records were excluded offline last round. What is left is outside both, and
# the next step for it is the vendor capture image running geom/a_lin, whose
# out_zp is 145 and whose output is linear, which answers whether this hardware
# can put a value below its output zero point at all. That is a different card
# and not this round.
#
# ⚠ WHAT ROUND 221 DID NOT SETTLE, and I nearly wrote that it did. MobileNet
# with ROCKET_WHOLE_GRAPH=1 came back 1000 of 1001, item for item identical to
# the run with the gate off, and no stall. That is equally consistent with the
# whole graph path running correctly and with it never engaging, because
# rkt_pack_graph_regcmd returns early on several conditions and leaves
# graph_regcmd NULL, and the normal path then runs exactly as before. Nothing
# in the log separates those. Round 215 shipped an unobservable knob once
# already.
#
# So the knob says out loud what it did. ROCKET_JOB_STATS prints the job count,
# the task count and whether the packed stream is in use, which is also the
# number Tomeu's question is about.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   gate off prints many jobs, gate on prints 1 job with the same task total
#             the whole graph path is real, it engages, and it computes. The
#             gate is stale and one job per graph can be the default.
#   both print the same job count
#             the path never engaged and last round's identical answer meant
#             nothing. The gate stays and the reason it stalls is still open.
#   gate on prints 1 job and the numbers move
#             it engages and it is wrong, which is the finding.
#
# The control is conv2d-cal, one operation, where both settings must print the
# same thing because there is nothing to pack.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 221. The stream is exhausted except for one comparison I
# have never actually made, so make it.
#
# WHAT ROUND 220 SETTLED. Both controls held. The identity override through
# ROCKET_REG_SET left conv2d-cal byte for byte unchanged, and forcing 0x4050
# back to the round 217 value printed "NPU job timed out" and 32 of 56, so the
# override is inert when it should be and reaches the hardware when it should.
#
#   entry 2   ROCKET_OUT_OFFSET=-64 gives maxdiff exactly 64 on every channel,
#             correlation 1.0000, and npu min exactly 64 with NOTHING below it.
#             The pixels pinned at out_zp follow the offset. So the clamp is
#             upstream of the offset and the store is not it.
#
#             ⚠ MY PREDICTION WAS HALF WRONG. I wrote "min 64, max 191, 128
#             distinct" and got max 246 and 159 distinct. The extra values are
#             all above 191, because at offset 0 the top of this model's range
#             was saturating at 255 and shifting down by 64 uncovers it. That
#             is the model's own range and not the clamp. The half that discriminates
#             was "nothing below 64", and it held.
#
#   entry 3   all four BS_CFG values including the explicit zero produced byte
#             identical output. The BS block is excluded. The control cannot
#             separate "zero is already the value" from "writes there do
#             nothing", but entry 5 proves DPU writes land and ROCKET_BS_CFG
#             goes through the same emitter.
#
#   entry 4   not discriminating, as expected. Turning a ReLU on where the
#             output is already ReLU'd is a no-op.
#
# WHAT THE OFFLINE WORK SETTLED SINCE, on the desktop and with no board.
#
#   The coefficient records do not carry the activation. geom/a_relu and
#   geom/a_lin are the same convolution with the same seed and their A, B and C
#   are identical on all 64 channels. The control a_lin2, linear with different
#   weights, moves A on 64 of 64, B on 63 and C on 64, so the comparison has
#   sensitivity. Output zero point and scale live in registers, not records.
#
#   The activation registers are 0x4060, 0x406c and 0x4074, confirmed with the
#   three way control the pair was built for, a register counting only if it
#   differs between relu and linear AND agrees between the two linears. Mesa
#   writes the linear value at all three.
#
#   Mesa writes the SAME REGISTER SET as the vendor in all four units, 139
#   against 138, the one extra being the broadcast OP_EN that engages the
#   units. So the clamp is not a register Mesa fails to write.
#
# THE GAP. I have never compared VALUES for the same model. The Mesa stream on
# disk is from an unknown model, its 0x40ac is ffffff80 which means out_zp 0
# while conv2d-cal is 128, so its twelve differences say nothing. The vendor
# compiled conv2d-cal's exact geometry as geom/g_cal, and ROCKET_DUMP_REGCMD
# already prints the assembled stream to the console, so the comparison costs
# one boot.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   conv2d-cal's stream prints and correctness is unchanged at 128 of 128
#             the dump does not perturb the run and the diff can be trusted.
#             If correctness moves, the instrument changed the experiment and
#             nothing else counts.
#   the diff against g_cal comes back clean apart from addresses and the
#   model's own requant
#             the register stream is fully exhausted, and what is left is
#             outside it. The next step is the vendor capture image running
#             geom/a_lin, whose out_zp is 145 and whose output is linear, which
#             answers whether this hardware can put a value below its output
#             zero point at all.
#   some register differs
#             that is the answer, and it was one boot away for four months.
#
# The zero point 0 model is dumped beside it so the two can be diffed against
# each other as well, which is what the comment on ROCKET_DUMP_REGCMD says the
# instrument is for.
#
# ONE MORE THING, unrelated to the clamp and marked as such. The whole graph
# path, rkt_pack_graph_regcmd, packs every task of every operation into ONE PC
# job the way the vendor dispatches. It is gated on ROCKET_WHOLE_GRAPH and off
# by default, and the comment above it blames the unit arming for the stall
# that closed it. That reading predates the PC task number finding, where a
# field layout meant for RK3588 asked this PC for 28673 tasks; the boot line
# below sets task_con16, which is the fix. So the gate may be stale and nobody
# has looked since. It runs LAST because a stall costs the entries after it,
# and it needs a multi operation graph to reach the path at all.
#
#   MobileNet computes with the gate on
#             the gate is stale and one job per graph can be the default,
#             which is the question waiting on Tomeu.
#   it stalls or the numbers move
#             the arming reading was right after all, or there is a second
#             cause, and the gate stays.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 220. Half of every symmetric surface is gone, and it is a
# clamp, so find the clamp.
#
# WHAT ROUND 219 MEASURED. The control held: all five out_zp 0 models printed
# "the clamp moves no pixel here", which is what a uint8 surface must do. The
# fourth branch of the rule fired on everything else, four times over with
# identical pixel counts:
#
#   conv2d-cal   clamp rewrites 100820 of 204800 pixels, 128 of 128 channels
#                vs UNCLAMPED cpu    0 of 128 channels match
#                vs max(cpu,zp)    128 of 128 channels match
#   cal_k1       94231 of 204800    1 of 128  vs  128 of 128
#   cal_k3       98391 of 204800    0 of 128  vs  128 of 128
#   cal_oc16     12566 of 25600     0 of 16   vs   16 of 16
#
# The npu surface equals max(cpu, out_zp) to within one EVERYWHERE, the raw row
# dump included. So the arithmetic above the zero point is exact and the whole
# half below it lands on exactly out_zp. That is a clamp, not an error.
#
# WHAT IT IS, in the hardware's own terms. out_offset is out_zp - 0x80, so the
# output conversion adds 0x80 of its own and the byte is 0x80 plus a signed
# result r. conv2d-cal has out_zp 128, offset 0, and shows min 128 max 255,
# exactly 128 distinct values: r is 0 to 127 and never negative. mn_conv0 has
# out_zp 0, offset -128, and reaches 255, so r is not limited to 127 either.
# Both readings agree on one statement and only one: r is clamped at zero.
#
# On an out_zp 0 model that clamp is exactly what the model wants, because its
# ReLU6 was folded into the quantisation range. That is why it has been
# invisible for four months, and why every green number in the regression set
# is green.
#
# WHERE IT IS NOT. Mesa's DPU stream was diffed register by register against
# the vendor's own linear model this round. Every register the vendor writes,
# Mesa writes; Mesa writes none the vendor does not; and the only differences
# are the geometry and the model's own requant. All five clamp bound pairs
# (0x4058/0x405c, 0x406c/0x4070, 0x4074/0x4078, 0x4088/0x408c, 0x40a4/0x40a8)
# are 80000000 and 7fffffff on both sides, wide open. The activation lives at
# 0x4060 bit 0 with 0x406c and 0x4074, from the a_lin and a_relu pair, and Mesa
# already emits the linear value at all three. And 0x4040 is written by NOBODY:
# scanned across all 60 vendor .rknn files in the tree, it never appears.
#
# So the clamp is not in any register that has been read, and asking about one
# more register has been costing a rebuild. ROCKET_REG_SET fixes that: a late
# comma separated list of reg=val emitted after everything else, target taken
# from the address. Inert when unset.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   1 the knob is inert. An override writing the value Mesa already writes must
#     leave conv2d-cal at 128 of 128 with the same 100820 pixel footprint. If it
#     does not, the override corrupts the stream and nothing else counts.
#
#   2 WHERE the clamp sits relative to the output offset. ROCKET_OUT_OFFSET
#     moves the addend without touching anything else.
#       min lands exactly at 64 with 128 distinct
#                 r is clamped at zero upstream of the offset, the store is not
#                 the clamp, and the search is inside the datapath.
#       values appear below 64, distinct above 128
#                 the negative half survives to the store and only the byte
#                 conversion loses it, which is a different and much smaller
#                 fix: the offset and the sign convention.
#
#   3 the BS stage, the one block nobody writes. ROCKET_BS_CFG=0 is the control:
#     if writing zero changes nothing, zero is what the register already holds
#     and the sweep above it means something. If writing zero DOES change
#     something, the reset value is not zero and that is the finding.
#       any value makes conv2d-cal's npu min drop below 128
#                 the clamp is found. The line to read is "vs UNCLAMPED cpu"
#                 rising off 0 of 128, not the old column.
#       nothing moves under any of them
#                 the BS block is not it, and what is left is the coefficient
#                 records rather than the register stream.
#
#   4 the reach control, LAST because it is destructive. Forcing 0x4050 back to
#     the round 217 value through ROCKET_REG_SET must reproduce the pw64x56w56
#     timeout. If that shape comes back 56 of 56, the override never reached the
#     hardware and entries 2 and 3 say nothing about anything.
#
# ⚠ READ ENTRY 1 AND ENTRY 4 BEFORE ANY OTHER LINE. They are the two ends of
# the same question, whether ROCKET_REG_SET does exactly what it claims and
# nothing else.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 219. The reference itself has never been measured.
#
# ROUND 218 CLOSED THE LAST BROKEN SHAPE. pw64x56w56 went from timing out in
# every round it ever ran to 56 of 56 every channel correct, and the controls
# held. Every shape in the regression set now computes. So the question is no
# longer which shape fails. It is whether the number that says they pass means
# what it has been read to mean.
#
# WHAT perch.py COMPARES AGAINST. Since June the reference has been
# max(cpu, out_zp), on the grounds that the output min pins at out_zp in every
# run as if the accumulator were being ReLU'd. That clamp has never itself been
# measured. It is free on an operator whose quantised output range starts at
# zero, because a uint8 pixel cannot go below zero. On an operator with out_zp
# in the middle of the range it rewrites every pixel below it, and two surfaces
# flattened the same way agree without either having computed anything.
#
# READ OFFLINE THIS ROUND, out of the model files rather than off the board:
#
#   every model in the tree is fused_activation NONE, MobileNet included. Its
#   ReLU6 is folded into the output quantisation range instead, which is why
#   those layers carry out_zp 0 and the clamp costs nothing there.
#
#   of the six models in the regression set only conv2d-cal has out_zp 128.
#   The other five are 0.
#
#   the whole cal_* family, the one the int8 convolution was declared byte
#   exact on in June, is out_zp 128 with fused_activation NONE.
#
# So the model the central correctness claim rests on is exactly the one where
# the reference is doing work, and how much has never been asked.
#
# WHAT THE VENDOR DOES, from a pair of its own compiled models. geom/a_lin and
# geom/a_relu are the same convolution with and without the activation, and
# their register streams differ in three places. BS_CFG at 0x4040 is not one of
# them. Neither stream writes it at all.
#
#     0x4060   linear 00000903   relu 00000902
#     0x406c   linear 80000000   relu 00000000
#     0x4074   linear 80000000   relu 00000000
#
# 0x406c and 0x4074 are paired with 0x4070 and 0x4078 at 7fffffff, so they are
# clamp bounds and the activation is the lower one moving from INT32_MIN to
# zero. Mesa emits the LINEAR value at all three, and so does the vendor in its
# own capture for a middle zero point model. So nothing in the register stream
# is asking for a ReLU, and the output min still sits at out_zp. One of those
# two statements is wrong and the measurement says which.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN. perch.py now scores both ways and
# prints the clamp's footprint in pixels and in channels.
#
#   a zero point model reports a nonzero footprint
#             the reading above is wrong, a uint8 surface went below zero, and
#             nothing else in this log counts.
#   conv2d-cal reports no footprint
#             the reference was never doing work here either. Every earlier
#             number stands unchanged and this thread closes.
#   a footprint, and the unclamped score near the clamped one
#             the hardware reproduces the real convolution and the clamp is
#             harmless. The numbers stand and the thread closes.
#   a footprint, and the unclamped score far below
#             the hardware is clamping something no model asked for, and the
#             June byte exact result is scored against a reference that hides
#             it. What comes next is already named: the vendor moves the lower
#             clamp bound for an activation and Mesa already writes the linear
#             value there, so whatever clamps is not in the part of the stream
#             that has been read.
#
# ⚠ NOTHING BUT perch.py CHANGED. Mesa, the kernel and charsiu are byte for
# byte round 218. Any movement in a channel count is this file or the scoring,
# not the hardware, and the clamped column prints beside the new one so the two
# are comparable in the same log.
#
# Checked on the host before flashing, against the shipped text rather than a
# copy of it, at 1x1x1001 with zero point 0 and with 128, at 5x5x8 with the
# hardware clamped and at 56x56x64 with the hardware unclamped. All four print.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 218. One register is left, and it was fitted on a sweep that
# never contained the failing case.
#
# ROUND 217. The reshape printed `56x56 -> 1x3136`, the control pw64x41w56 at
# one column stayed 41 of 41, and pw64x56w56 at one column STILL TIMED OUT. So
# the axis is not the fault either, which was the rule's second branch and it
# named what comes next.
#
# WITH THE GEOMETRY MATCHED, the diff against charsiu's own stream for the same
# shape and the same channel counts comes down to:
#
#   6 addresses, 3 requant registers, and ONE other:
#
#     0x4050    charsiu 80011111    mesa 80011011
#
# charsiu computes this shape. Mesa cannot. They now differ in one register.
#
# WHERE MESA'S VALUE COMES FROM, and it is in the driver's own comment: ten
# vendor .rknn files compiled at output channel counts from 16 to 160 IN STEPS
# OF 16, giving 0x80011111 for every multiple of 32 and 0x80011011 otherwise,
# 10 out of 10. The rule written from that was `oc % 32 == 0`.
#
# ⚠ EVERY ONE OF THOSE TEN POINTS IS A MULTIPLE OF 16, and 56 is not. On that
# sweep `oc % 32 == 0` and `DIV_ROUND_UP(oc,16) is even` are the SAME PREDICATE
# and cannot be told apart. They part company exactly where oc is a multiple of
# neither 32 nor 16, and 56 is such a count: DIV_ROUND_UP(56,16) = 4, even, so
# the parity form says 0x80011111, which is what charsiu emits and what
# computes.
#
# Checked offline before flashing: the two forms agree at 16, 32, 48, 64, 80,
# 96, 112, 128, 144 and 160, so nothing the vendor sweep measured moves. On this
# board only oc 56 changes value. 41 stays 0x80011011, 64 and 128 stay
# 0x80011111.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   pw64x56w56 stops timing out
#             the predicate was fitted on a subset that could not distinguish
#             it, and the parity form is right. The last plainly broken shape
#             on this driver closes.
#   it still times out
#             0x4050 is not it either, and with the streams now identical bar
#             addresses, what is left is a buffer or an address rather than
#             anything in the stream.
#   pw64x41w56, pw33x64w56 or conv2d-cal moves
#             the two forms are not equivalent after all, which the offline
#             check says they are, so that would mean the check is wrong. All
#             three run FIRST.
#
# ROCKET_DPU4050_MOD32 restores the modulo form, so both are in one log.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 217. The knob works, and I had its parameter backwards in
# two round headers.
#
# ROUND 216 CONFIRMED THE MECHANISM. `rocket: PW_RESHAPE 56x56 -> 3136x1`
# printed, and the dumps with the knob off and on differ in THIRTEEN registers,
# with 0x37 (=55) becoming 0xc3f (=3135) and 0x38 becoming 0xc40. So the
# hardware really is given the new geometry, and round 215's result stands: the
# spatial shape is not the fault, since every reshape still timed out.
#
# ⚠ EXCEPT THAT NONE OF THOSE RESHAPES WAS charsiu's GEOMETRY. I wrote
# "3136x1, which is charsiu's geometry exactly" in two headers. It is the
# TRANSPOSE of it. ROCKET_PW_RESHAPE=W sets the WIDTH, so =3136 is 3136 columns
# by 1 row, while charsiu runs M=3136 ROWS by 1 column. The sweep covered 16,
# 28, 112 and 3136 columns and never once covered ONE column.
#
# The diff says so plainly. Against charsiu's own stream for the same shape and
# the same channel counts, with addresses and the requant triple set aside,
# only thirteen registers differ and twelve of them are that transposition:
#
#   0x102c  0x1078  0x118c  0x301c  0x4034      the two halves swapped
#   0x1030  0x103c  0x1044  0x4020  0x4024
#   0x500c  0x5010                              3136 in the other field
#
# and exactly ONE is something else:
#
#   0x4050   charsiu 80011111   mesa 80011011
#
# So the two stacks emit nearly the same stream for this work. What separates
# them is which axis carries the 3136, and one nibble in 0x4050.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   ROCKET_PW_RESHAPE=1 completes without a timeout
#             the axis is the fault. Mesa asks for 3136 columns of one row
#             where the hardware wants one column of 3136 rows, and 56x56 is
#             the same fault in a less obvious form.
#   it still times out
#             the axis is not it either, and 0x4050 is the only difference
#             left standing, which makes it the next and nearly the last
#             thing to try.
#   it completes but the answer is wrong
#             expected and fine. The reshaped output lands where perch.py does
#             not look; only the timeout is being read.
#
# THE CONTROL: pw64x41w56 at the same reshape. It works unreshaped and it
# worked at 3136 columns, so if one column breaks it, the mechanism and not the
# finding is at fault.
#
# ⚠ Note for the record: the dump in round 216's entry 3 was pw64x41w56, and I
# first diffed it against charsiu at N=56. Wrong channel count, wrong
# comparison. Redone against N=41 offline before writing any of the above.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 216. Round 215 asked a question its own instrument could not
# answer, and the fault is mine and was avoidable.
#
# WHAT ROUND 215 SHOWED. Every reshape of pw64x56w56, at 3136x1, 112x28, 28x112
# and 16x196, still timed out at 32 of 56. The control, pw64x41w56 reshaped to
# 3136x1, came back 41 of 41 correct.
#
# ⚠ AND THAT CONTROL PROVES NOTHING, WHICH I SHOULD HAVE SEEN WHEN WRITING IT.
# A 1x1 convolution's output surface is laid out identically whether it is
# called 56x56 or 3136x1, because the pixels are in the same order either way.
# So a correct answer is what you get BOTH if the reshape worked AND if it
# never ran. The two cases are indistinguishable in that log, and the whole of
# entry 3 is therefore unreadable, exactly like round 208.
#
# The rule I keep having to relearn: verifying that a knob's STRING is in the
# binary is not verifying that the knob DOES anything. Round 205 checked
# strings and shipped a crash; this round checked strings and shipped a knob
# whose effect could not be observed.
#
# SO THE KNOB SAYS SO ITSELF NOW. It prints
#
#   rocket: PW_RESHAPE 56x56 -> 3136x1
#
# to stderr when it fires, and the same run's register stream is dumped so the
# geometry can be confirmed in the values the hardware is actually given rather
# than inferred from an answer that cannot vary.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   the PW_RESHAPE line appears and the dumped geometry registers change
#             the knob works, and then round 215's result stands: the spatial
#             shape is NOT the fault, since every reshape still timed out.
#   the line does not appear
#             the knob never ran, round 215 said nothing at all, and the
#             condition guarding it is what to fix.
#   the line appears but the geometry registers are unchanged
#             it fires and is then overwritten downstream, which is worth more
#             than either of the above.
#
# THE CONTROL: with the knob OFF, the same dump must show 56x56 geometry, so
# the comparison has both sides in one log.
#
# ⚠ WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE either way: charsiu computes 64 in and 56 out over
# 3136 rows to 175358 of 175616. Mesa cannot complete the same arithmetic. That
# gap is real whatever this knob turns out to do.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 215. The output rounding was measured and is not the answer,
# so it is withdrawn, and the only variable left is the spatial shape.
#
# ROUND 214: four controls every channel correct, including pw64x41w56 with its
# oc_pad moved from 42 to 48 and pw33x64w56 whose oc is already 64. And
# pw64x56w56 timed out at 32 of 56 with the atom rounding, which is BYTE FOR
# BYTE what it does with the pair rounding in the same log. So rounding the
# output count to the feature atom breaks nothing and fixes nothing.
#
# IT IS WITHDRAWN FROM THE DEFAULT. A change with no evidence behind it costs
# weight buffer and clouds the next measurement, so ROCKET_OC_ATOM keeps it
# reachable and the default goes back to the pair. The asymmetry with the input
# side is real and is now known NOT to be the fault, which is worth as much as
# a fix would have been.
#
# The recovery discipline also proved itself: attempt 1 came back 0 of 128 and
# attempts 2 and 3 came back 128 of 128, which is exactly why round 186 and
# round 213 both lost entries to using a single run.
#
# WHAT IS LEFT, and it is now the only thing. charsiu computes 64 in and 56 out
# over 3136 ROWS to 175358 of 175616 with nothing off by more than one; Mesa
# cannot complete the same arithmetic over a 56x56 SURFACE. A 1x1 convolution
# does not read its neighbours, so those two are the same computation. Ruled
# out so far: the register stream (211), the output buffer and the hardware
# itself (212), the input CBUF budget at six settings (213), the output channel
# rounding (214).
#
# ROCKET_PW_RESHAPE=W re-expresses a pointwise layer's surface as W columns
# with W * H held constant. 56x56 becomes 3136x1, 112x28, 28x112 and so on.
#
# ⚠ THE ANSWER WILL BE WRONG UNDER A RESHAPE and that is expected: the output
# lands in a differently laid out surface, so perch.py will compare the wrong
# pixels. THE ONLY THING BEING READ IS WHETHER THE JOB COMPLETES. A reshape
# that stops the timeout says the geometry is the fault even with a wrong
# answer, and that is why the entries below are read for "timed out" and not
# for channel counts.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   some reshape completes without a timeout
#             the spatial shape is the fault, and which reshapes work bounds
#             it. 3136x1 completing would mean charsiu's geometry is simply
#             one Mesa never asks for.
#   every reshape still times out
#             it is not the surface either, and the difference between charsiu
#             and Mesa on this shape is somewhere neither the stream nor the
#             geometry reaches.
#   the reshape breaks pw64x41w56 as well
#             the reshape code is wrong rather than the finding, and entry 2 is
#             there to catch exactly that.
#
# THE CONTROL: pw64x41w56 reshaped the same way. It works unreshaped, so if it
# times out reshaped, the mechanism is at fault and nothing below can be read.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 214. The input count rounds to the feature atom; the output
# count has only ever rounded to a pair. That asymmetry is the last thing left
# that fits the evidence.
#
# ROUND 213: EVERY BUDGET TIMED OUT. Default, 2048, 1024, 512 entries and row
# windows of 28 and 14, all of them, and the small budgets made it WORSE,
# taking pw64x56w56 from 32 of 56 to 0 of 56, while pw64x41w56 stayed 41 of 41
# at every one. So it is not the input CBUF capacity, which was the rule's
# second branch.
#
# ⚠ AND A DESIGN FAULT OF MINE, THE SAME ONE AS ROUND 186. Every block's first
# line, conv2d-cal, came back 0 of 128 except in the first block, because it
# runs right after the previous block's timeout and the entry after a timeout
# has been poisoned since round 138. I used ONE run as a recovery check when
# round 190 had already established that recovery needs retrying. pw64x41w56,
# the second line, passed in every block and is what makes the third line
# readable at all.
#
# WHAT NOW FITS. Three pointwise shapes on the same 56x56 surface:
#
#   33 in, 64 out   correct     oc_pad 64
#   64 in, 41 out   correct     oc_pad 42
#   64 in, 56 out   TIMES OUT   oc_pad 56
#
# and everything else is ruled out: the hardware computes 56 output channels
# over 3136 rows (round 212), the two streams differ only in values carrying
# the output count (211), the output buffer is twice the size it needs (212),
# and the input CBUF budget is not it (213).
#
# The input side needed ALIGN(ic, 16) in three places and the output side has
# only ever had ALIGN(oc, 2). This round gives it ALIGN(oc, 16) in the two
# places that describe the weight buffer: its size, its layout, and the count
# the CNA is told. The REAL output count still goes to CORE 0x3020, DPU 0x402c
# and RDMA 0x5014, so no extra channel is written.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   pw64x56w56 stops timing out
#             the output count needed the atom too, and the symmetry with the
#             input side is complete.
#   it still times out
#             the atom is not it either, and what is left is that a 2D surface
#             does something a 3136 row one does not, since charsiu computes
#             the same arithmetic.
#   pw64x41w56 or pw33x64w56 changes
#             the padding reached a shape it must not. Both run BEFORE the test
#             and pw33x64w56 has oc 64, already a multiple of 16, so it cannot
#             legitimately move at all.
#
# ROCKET_OC_PAIR restores the pair rounding, so before and after are in one
# log, and the recovery step is three runs of conv2d-cal rather than one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 213. The hardware can do the shape, so pw64x56w56's timeout
# is Mesa's, and this round asks whether it is a capacity by turning the one
# knob that changes capacity.
#
# ROUND 212, control passing: three runs of M=3136 K=64 N=64 all gave
# 200394 of 200704, identical.
#
#   N=56 at M=3136   two of three runs 175358 of 175616, 0 off by more than 1
#   N=41 at M=3136   three of three wrong, two with a timeout, and the two
#                    non-timeout results are byte identical to each other
#
# **The hardware computes 56 output channels over 3136 rows.** So pw64x56w56's
# timeout is Mesa asking wrongly, not a hardware limit.
#
# charsiu's own N=41 failure is the OPPOSITE pairing from Mesa, which gets 41
# right and 56 wrong, so the two are not the same fault. 41 is odd, Mesa has an
# oc_pad for that and charsiu only applies ALIGN_UP(n,2) to the weights while
# its output surface uses ALIGN(N,16). That is charsiu's own bug and it is
# recorded, not chased.
#
# WHAT THE STREAM AND THE BUFFERS RULE OUT. Round 211's diff was seven values,
# all carrying the output count, plus 0x5024 which is the coefficient address,
# so there is no semantic difference. The output buffer is not short either:
# calc_raw_output_size gives 56*56*DIV_ROUND_UP(56,16)*2*16 = 401408 bytes where
# ALIGN(56,16) needs 200704, twice over.
#
# WHAT IS LEFT. The input CBUF budget is computed from the INPUT
# (entries_per_slice * input_height), and these two shapes have identical
# inputs, 64 channels over 56x56, and identical 147 entry streams. So the
# splitting is the same for both, and conv2d-cal at 40x40x128 stages a wider
# row than either and is correct. None of that explains it, which is why the
# knob gets turned instead of the reasoning continued.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   a smaller ROCKET_CBUF_ENTRIES makes the timeout go away
#             it is a capacity, the budget is too large for this shape, and the
#             value at which it starts working bounds where the real limit is.
#   it times out at every budget
#             not the input CBUF, and the next place is the output side, where
#             nothing in this driver currently caps anything.
#   it stops timing out but the answer is still wrong
#             two faults, and the timeout was hiding the second.
#
# THE CONTROL: conv2d-cal and pw64x41w56 run under the SAME reduced budget and
# must stay every channel correct. A budget small enough to break them would
# make any improvement in pw64x56w56 unreadable. And each configuration is
# preceded by a known good model, because the entry after a timeout has been
# poisoned since round 138.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 212. The two streams differ only in geometry, so the stream
# is not what is wrong, and the question moves to whether the hardware can do
# this shape at all.
#
# ROUND 211'S DIFF. Both dumps are 143 entries with the same registers, and
# exactly seven values differ:
#
#   0x101c   2688 = 64*42          3584 = 64*56       ic * oc_pad
#   0x1024   41 (= 42 - 1)         55 (= 56 - 1)
#   0x3020   40 (= 41 - 1)         55                 oc_real - 1
#   0x402c   40                    55
#   0x4030   high 40               high 55
#   0x5014   40                    55
#   0x5024   12768                 12848              differs by 80
#
# Every one of them carries the output channel count. There is NO non geometry
# difference, which was the branch of the written rule that would have been the
# stronger lead. And the timeout still let the dump be written, so the stream
# reaches the buffer and the fault is in executing it.
#
# So the stream describes N = 56 correctly and the hardware times out anyway.
# charsiu computes N = 56 exactly, at one row and at eight, which is why the
# channel count was ruled out in round 209. What charsiu has NEVER run is
# N = 56 at M = 3136, and 3136 is 56 * 56, which is exactly the work Mesa is
# asking for.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   charsiu computes M=3136 N=56
#             the hardware can do the shape, Mesa is asking wrongly, and since
#             the streams differ only in geometry the fault is in a buffer or
#             an address rather than a register value.
#   charsiu times out too
#             the shape itself is what the hardware or the driver cannot do at
#             this size, which is a rocket level finding rather than a Mesa one
#             and is worth reporting upstream on its own.
#   charsiu is flaky on it
#             then it belongs with round 210's unexplained flakiness rather
#             than with pw64x56w56, and the two get chased together or not at
#             all.
#
# EVERY POINT RUNS THREE TIMES. Round 210 established that charsiu's N path is
# flaky when N moves between runs, so a single run of any N is not evidence
# now. N = 41 is in as the count Mesa gets RIGHT at this surface, and N = 64 as
# the baseline. If the three N = 64 runs are not all identical, nothing else in
# the entry can be read.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 211. The prediction was refuted and the mistake was mine
# before the board ever ran it. Back to the one shape that is still plainly
# broken.
#
# ROUND 210, and the control is the good news: four identical runs were all
# byte exact, so the device does survive repetition and no sweep in this tree
# is suspect for that reason.
#
#   A, growing   N=16 ok   N=40 FAIL   N=56 ok   N=64 ok
#   B, shrinking N=64 ok   N=56 ok     N=40 ok   N=16 FAIL
#
# The prediction was "growing all pass, shrinking all fail after the first".
# Both halves are wrong, and the same N=40 failed in one order and passed in
# the other, so the buffer shrinking is NOT it.
#
# ⚠ THE FAILURE WAS MINE AND IT WAS BEFORE THE RUN. Round 209's seven points
# fit TWO rules equally well: "the buffer got smaller than the last one" and
# "shapes with ALIGN(N,16) of 48 or less are unstable". I wrote down only the
# first and tested only the first. The second is what round 210's data leans
# towards, since N=16 and N=40 each failed once and passed once while N=56 and
# N=64 never failed, but that is another induction and it now has to carry a
# third fact: it is FLAKY. The same shape does both.
#
# WHAT THAT DOES AND DOES NOT COST. It appears only when N MOVES between runs.
# Every fixed shape result in this tree has been stable: M=1 N=64, the whole K
# sweep, M=3136 K=33 N=64 at 200344 of 200704, and every Mesa model, which all
# have one shape each. Round 209 already showed the output channel count itself
# is fine, N = 16, 40, 41, 56 and 63 all byte exact at one row. So this is
# charsiu's N path being flaky, not a fault in the hardware or in Mesa, and it
# is left recorded rather than chased while MobileNet is the priority.
#
# BACK TO THE ONE SHAPE STILL PLAINLY BROKEN. pw64x56w56 is 32 of 56 and times
# out every round. Its output count is 56, and charsiu computes N = 56 exactly
# at one row and at eight, so it is not the channel count. Round 201's method
# is what worked last time: dump this driver's own register stream for the
# broken shape and for a working one and diff them offline.
#
# pw64x41w56 is the right thing to diff against: same input channels, same
# 56x56 surface, same 1x1 kernel, and every channel correct. The only geometry
# that differs is the output count, 41 against 56.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   the dumps differ in registers that carry the output count only
#             the fault is in one of those values and the diff names it.
#   they differ somewhere else as well
#             read that first: a difference that is not geometry is a stronger
#             lead than one that is.
#   the timeout happens before the stream is written
#             no dump appears, and that itself says the fault is earlier than
#             the register stream.
#
# THE CONTROL: pw64x41w56 must still be every channel correct in the same log,
# and the broken shape runs LAST as always, since the entry after a timeout has
# been poisoned since round 138.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 210. Round 209's baselines found something bigger than the
# question they were guarding, and this round tests it with the prediction
# written down first.
#
# ROUND 209: EVERY POINT WAS BYTE EXACT AT ONE ROW.
#
#   M=1  N=16 16/16   N=40 40/40   N=41 41/41   N=56 56/56   N=63 63/63
#   M=8  N=41 265/328 (the only wrong answer)   N=56 448/448
#
# So the output channel count is not the problem and round 208's zeros were all
# device state. pw64x56w56's fault is not N: charsiu computes N = 56 exactly at
# one row and at eight. And charsiu's M=8 N=41 is wrong while Mesa's N=41 shape
# is correct and its N=56 one is not, which is the OPPOSITE pairing, so those
# two faults are not the same thing.
#
# WHAT THE BASELINES SAID, and it is 7 for 7:
#
#   point      output BO         baseline AFTER it
#   N=16       16 * 1            0 of 64      broken
#   N=40       48 * 1            0 of 64      broken
#   N=41       48 * 1            0 of 64      broken
#   N=56       64 * 1           64 of 64      fine
#   N=63       64 * 1           64 of 64      fine
#   M=8 N=41   48 * 8            0 of 512     broken
#   M=8 N=56   64 * 8          512 of 512     fine
#
# The baseline's own buffer is 64 * M. Every point whose output buffer is
# SMALLER than the baseline's breaks the run after it; every point whose buffer
# equals it does not. The baseline BEFORE each point was correct every single
# time, so whatever it is recovers on its own within one run.
#
# That is induced from seven points, which this project has a standing rule
# against trusting, so the prediction goes in before the board sees it.
#
# THE PREDICTION, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   A  buffers that only GROW:   N = 16, 40, 56, 64   all four byte exact
#   B  buffers that only SHRINK: N = 64, 56, 40, 16   the first is byte exact
#                                and every one after it fails
#   C  the same buffer four times: N = 64 x 4         all four byte exact
#
# THE DECISION RULE.
#
#   A passes, B fails after its first, C passes
#             it is the buffer SHRINKING between submits, not the shape, and
#             that is a driver level finding rather than a charsiu one.
#   A and B both pass
#             the rule induced from round 209 is wrong and the seven points had
#             another cause, most likely order or time rather than size.
#   C fails
#             the device does not survive four identical runs, which would make
#             every sweep in this tree suspect and matters more than any of it.
#   B passes but A fails
#             the direction is backwards from the induction, which would be the
#             most interesting outcome of the three.
#
# Each sequence runs in one shot with no baseline between its steps, because
# the baseline is what would perturb the very thing being measured.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 209. Round 208's sweep is VOID by its own control, and the
# way it failed is the finding: the device does not stay good across runs when
# N moves.
#
# THE CONTROL THAT KILLED IT. The same command, M = 1 K = 64 N = 40, ran twice
# in one log on one boot:
#
#   entry 1    40 of 40 bytes written, 40 BYTE EXACT
#   entry 2     0 of 40 bytes written,  0 BYTE EXACT
#
# and N = 16, which is a multiple of the atom and had to be right, came back
# 0 of 16. Five "NPU job timed out" lines are scattered through the log, one of
# them between the start of a run and its own successful result. So every
# "0 of X bytes written" in that sweep is device state and not a property of N,
# and no rule about which N works can be read from it. It is not read.
#
# Round 195's sweep of K did not do this. The difference is that K moves the
# weight buffer while N moves the OUTPUT buffer, whose size this project
# changed in round 200 to ALIGN(N, atom) * M, and whose surface layout puts
# channel n of row m at (n/atom)*M*atom + m*atom + n%atom.
#
# SO EVERY POINT CARRIES ITS OWN BASELINE THIS TIME. For each N: a known good
# shape, then the shape under test, then the known good shape again. A point
# counts only if the baseline was correct on BOTH sides of it. That is the
# recover() discipline rounds 190 and 191 used on the wedge, applied to a sweep.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   a point whose baselines both pass and which is byte exact
#             that N works, and it is the first N result this project can
#             actually stand behind.
#   a point whose baselines both pass and which is not byte exact
#             that N is genuinely wrong.
#   a point whose SECOND baseline fails
#             that N breaks the device, which is a stronger statement than
#             being wrong and is what pw64x56w56's timeout probably is.
#   a point whose FIRST baseline fails
#             the damage came from before it; the point says nothing and the
#             blame belongs to the previous N.
#
# ⚠ Still refuted before the round starts: "N must be a multiple of 16".
# pw64x41w56 has 41 output channels and is every channel correct on Mesa. What
# separates 41 from 56 is the question; it is not the atom alone.
#
# The N set is smaller because each point now costs three runs. 16 is in as the
# case that must work, 40 and 56 are both 8 mod 16, 41 is Mesa's working odd
# count, and 63 is one short of the atom.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 208. The tool is finally out of the way, so ask the OUTPUT
# channel count the question that the input one answered in round 195.
#
# ROUND 207: no traceback anywhere, four controls at every channel correct, and
# the two MobileNet runs IDENTICAL now that the repeat is back in front of the
# timing out shape. The bigger branch of that rule did not fire: the model is
# deterministic on this driver.
#
# MOBILENET IN FULL, for the first time readable:
#
#   1000 of 1001 channels match, the one exception is ch 905 at maxdiff 4
#   every pixel is within 4, 12 channels are off by exactly 1 and 75% of those
#   have the npu LOW, which is the shape of rounding
#
# So 988 exact, 12 off by one, one off by four. That is where MobileNet stands.
#
# WHAT IS STILL PLAINLY WRONG is pw64x56w56: 32 of 56, and it times out. Its
# OUTPUT channel count is 56, and 56 is not a multiple of 16. The whole chain
# that fixed pw33x64w56 was about the INPUT count not being a multiple of the
# feature atom, and the output side has never been swept at all.
#
# ⚠ IT IS NOT A SIMPLE MIRROR, and the counter-example is already in the log:
# pw64x41w56 has 41 output channels, 41 is not a multiple of 16 either, and it
# is every channel correct. So "output must be a multiple of 16" is refuted
# before the round starts. Something else separates 41 from 56 and this round
# is for finding what, not for confirming a guess.
#
# charsiu asks it the same way round 195 asked about K, and it can now do it at
# M above 1 as well, which is where its own N = 40 came back writing NOTHING.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   byte exact exactly when N is a multiple of 16
#             then pw64x41w56 at 41 has to be explained, because it contradicts
#             that, and the explanation matters more than the rule.
#   byte exact except at particular N
#             read WHICH. 40 and 56 are both 8 mod 16; 33 and 41 are both 1 mod
#             8; 63 is -1. Those three groups failing differently would name it.
#   the failures depend on M as well as N
#             the output surface is [n/atom][m][n%atom], so an N off the atom
#             changes where every row after the first lands, and M = 1 could be
#             right while M = 8 is not for that reason alone.
#   everything is byte exact
#             the N side is fine and pw64x56w56's fault is elsewhere, which is
#             worth knowing for the price of one sweep.
#
# THE CONTROL: N = 64 and N = 16 at both M, which must be byte exact, and
# M = 1 N = 40, which round 200 already took from writing nothing to 40 of 40.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 207. Third crash from the same assumption, so this time all
# three places got found at once, and a second thing in round 206 was my own
# script ordering.
#
# ROUND 206: four controls at every channel correct again, pw33x64w56 at 64 of
# 64, and `interior EMPTY` printed where round 205 crashed. Then it crashed
# somewhere else, at `got[y, 1:17, wc]`, which is an IndexError rather than an
# empty reduction but the same assumption underneath: THAT THERE IS A SURFACE.
# MobileNet's classifier is 1x1x1001 and has no row 1, no row 2 and no column
# 16.
#
# Round 206's own written rule for a third crash was to stop patching call
# sites and find every one at once. Done: three places index space, and all
# three are now guarded and listed in a comment beside the last one.
#
#   ~156  inner maxdiff     prints -1
#   ~299  interior stats    falls back to the whole surface
#   ~352  the RAW row dump  prints the whole channel instead
#
# Checked on the host at 1x1x1001, 5x5x8 and 56x56x64 before flashing. All
# three pass. That check costs nothing and would have caught all three of these
# rounds ago.
#
# AND THE SECOND MOBILENET RUN IN ROUND 206 WAS UNREADABLE, BY MY OWN SCRIPT'S
# DESIGN. It came back 983 of 1001 with npu at ONE distinct value, against 1000
# of 1001 and nine distinct in the first run of the same model in the same log.
# The difference is that I put it AFTER pw64x56w56, which times out, and the
# entry after a timeout has come back poisoned since round 138. That is why the
# timing out shape has run LAST in this script for seventy rounds, and this
# round I inserted an entry behind it. The repeat is moved back in front.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   MobileNet prints every line, twice, with no traceback and the two runs
#   agree
#             the tool is finally out of the way and the remaining fault is one
#             channel of 1001 at maxdiff 4.
#   the two MobileNet runs disagree while both are BEFORE the timeout
#             the model is not deterministic on this driver, which would be a
#             much bigger finding than the one channel.
#   it crashes again
#             the assumption is somewhere a grep for got[ and ref[ does not
#             reach, and the next step is running perch.py on the host against
#             a saved 1x1 surface rather than reading it.
#
# Mesa, the kernel and charsiu are byte identical to round 205.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 206. The MobileNet regression set is clean and the last
# thing in the way of reading it is still my own tool.
#
# ROUND 205, all four controls at every channel correct:
#
#   mn_conv0      32 of 32     conv2d-cal    128 of 128
#   pw64x41w56    41 of 41     pw33x64w56     64 of 64
#
# So round 204's fix survived a rebuild. pw33x64w56 with ROCKET_NO_ICPAD_SUM
# came back 0 of 64 this time against 18 last round; both are simply wrong and
# the difference between two wrong runs is not information, but it is noted
# rather than passed over.
