#   pw33x64w56 is not 64 of 64
#             round 204 did not survive a rebuild and nothing else here counts.
#
# Nothing but perch.py changed this round. Mesa, the kernel and charsiu are
# byte identical to round 204, so any number that moves is the measurement.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 204. The dump ruled out two of the three records and the
# third one has a derivable correction, so this round derives it rather than
# fitting it.
#
# ROUND 203'S DUMP, and a design mistake of mine in it.
#
# The coefficient buffer is 64 byte records: [A 8 x i32][B 4 x i32][C 4 x i32].
# B is 0xFFFEFFFE and C is 0x00100010 in BOTH shapes, so neither carries the
# difference. A differs everywhere, and THAT MEANS NOTHING, because
# pw64x41w56 and pw33x64w56 are two different models with different weights and
# different biases. Diffing their A records was never going to say anything and
# I should have seen that before spending the entry on it.
#
# What the dump did confirm: mesa-weights is 3072 bytes, which is 48 * 64, so
# round 197's padded layout is really in the buffer.
#
# THE DERIVATION. calculate_weight_sum() walks the REAL input channels:
#
#   sw[oc] = sum over ic < 33 of (w_q - wt_zp)
#
# but since round 197 the buffer is laid out to ALIGN(ic, 16) = 48 and since
# round 202 the CNA is told 48, so the hardware accumulates over 48 channels.
# The padded weights are written as biased zero, which is w_q = 0x80, so each
# of the 15 padded channels contributes (0x80 - wt_zp) to the sum the hardware
# effectively uses, and this function missed all fifteen.
#
#   A[oc] = bias - (in_zp - 0x80) * sw[oc]
#
# so a short sw lands as ONE CONSTANT on every pixel of the channel, which is
# exactly the shape round 202 measured: slope 0.9997, offset -35.32 to -30.32,
# standard deviation 0.85.
#
# The correction is (padded - real) * (0x80 - wt_zp), added in the same units
# the function already returns. Nothing is fitted: no value was read off the
# residual and no constant was searched for. A shape whose ic is already a
# multiple of the atom pads by nothing and the term is identically zero.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   pw33x64w56 becomes correct
#             MobileNet's last open operation is closed.
#   the offset shrinks but does not vanish
#             the direction is right and something else contributes too; the
#             new offset's SIZE relative to -32.8 says how much of it this was.
#   the offset moves the wrong way or grows
#             the sign or the operand is wrong, and since both are derived
#             rather than fitted, that is worth more than a null result.
#   conv2d-cal, mn_conv0 or pw64x41w56 changes at all
#             the term is not zero where it must be. They run FIRST.
#
# ROCKET_NO_ICPAD_SUM drops the term so both are in one log.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 203. The five registers worked, and what is left is one
# constant that has been there since before them.
#
# ROUND 202, and the second branch of its written rule is what fired.
#
#                          slope a               b range          b std
#   five registers fixed    0.9997 (min 0.9721)  -35.32 .. -30.32   0.85
#   ROCKET_NO_ICPAD_REGS    0.9985 (min -0.4912) -212.88 .. +160.48 69.11
#
# The per channel offset's standard deviation collapsed from 69.11 to 0.85 and
# its worst slope went from -0.49 to 0.972. All three controls stayed at every
# channel correct, as they had to: their ic is already a multiple of 16 and the
# padding is the identity there.
#
# So pw33x64w56 is now one nearly global constant away: npu is about 32.8 above
# the reference everywhere. That constant is NOT from the registers. Round 199
# measured it at -31.41 with the weights already padded and the registers still
# on the real count, so it has been sitting underneath both fixes the whole
# time. charsiu computes the same arithmetic without it, which puts it in the
# coefficient buffer, the one part of the two stacks that is built rather than
# emitted.
#
# THIS ROUND DUMPS THAT BUFFER. ROCKET_DEBUG=dump_bos already writes
# mesa-biases-000-NNN.bin beside the regcmd, and the same od goes into the log
# for the failing shape and for pw64x41w56, which works. The A record is a per
# channel int32 and 33 versus 64 input channels changes what belongs in it, so
# two dumps of it are directly comparable in a way the geometry registers never
# were.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   the biases dump appears and the failing shape's A records differ from the
#   working one's by a constant
#             that constant is the answer and its size in accumulator counts
#             says which term it is.
#   the A records look right
#             the fault is in how the hardware CONSUMES the record at this ic,
#             not in what is written, and the next probe is charsiu building
#             the same coefficient buffer two ways.
#   the dump does not appear
#             biases are dumped from a different call site than the one this
#             shape takes, and entry 5's weights dump is the fallback.
#
# 32.8 output counts at shift 21 and scale 0x4301 is about 3913 accumulator
# counts, which is not obviously any of 15 * 128, 33 * 128 or 48 * 128, so it is
# NOT being guessed at here. The dump is what names it.
#
# THE CONTROL: the three shapes whose ic is a multiple of 16 run first and must
# still be every channel correct, and the failing shape is run once WITHOUT
# dump_bos so the dumps can be shown to be of the same run that produced the
# numbers.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 202. The dumped stream named five registers, and this is the
# change they asked for.
#
# ROUND 201 got Mesa's register stream off the board, 147 entries for a shape
# that works and 147 for the one that does not, same registers in both, 24
# values different. Most of those are geometry and mean nothing.
#
# The comparison that means something came from charsiu, offline and with no
# board: sweeping its K, which IS the input channel count, seven registers
# track it, and K = 33 and K = 48 emit IDENTICAL values in every one of them.
# That is what padding to the feature atom does. Laid against Mesa's failing
# stream:
#
#   reg      charsiu, computes to 0.025%   Mesa, COMPUTED 0
#   0x101c   0xc00 = 48 * 64                0x840 = 33 * 64
#   0x1020   0x30  = 48                     0x21  = 33
#   0x1028   low 0x2f = 47                  low 0x20 = 32
#   0x1030   high 0x60 = 2 * 48             high 0x42 = 2 * 33
#   0x107c   0x2f  = 47                     0x20  = 32
#
# All five come from one line, rkt_regcmd.c's `unsigned ic =
# task->input_channels_real`. rkt_task.c has padded task->input_channels to
# ALIGN(MAX2(ic, 16), 16) all along and rkt_fill_weights has laid the buffer out
# to that width since round 197, so these five were the last place still
# counting the real channels. Round 196 made exactly this change in charsiu and
# took K = 31, 33, 40, 63 and 65 from 30, 37, 25, 21 and 43 of 64 to 64 of 64.
#
# I ALSO GOT THE REGISTER SEMANTICS WRONG ONCE THIS ROUND and want it recorded:
# I read 0x1024 through rkt_registers.h as DATA_SIZE1 and started reasoning
# about its fields, but the working shape has 41 in it, which is its OUTPUT
# channel count. The pointwise path emits raw offsets rather than the RK3588
# derived REG_CNA_* map. The fix for that was not to guess harder but to sweep
# K in charsiu and let the registers that move name themselves.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   pw33x64w56 becomes correct
#             MobileNet's last open operation is closed and the driver's
#             pointwise path handles any input channel count.
#   its slope stays near 1 and the per channel offset shrinks
#             the registers were part of it and something in the coefficient
#             buffer is the rest. Round 199 measured that offset as VARYING per
#             channel, min -108 max +117, so its size is the thing to watch.
#   nothing moves
#             the five registers were not it, and the weight buffer contents
#             are next, dumped the same way the stream was.
#   conv2d-cal, mn_conv0 or pw64x41w56 breaks
#             the padding reached a shape whose ic is already a multiple of 16,
#             where ALIGN is the identity, so that could only be a coding
#             mistake. They run FIRST.
#
# ROCKET_NO_ICPAD_REGS restores the real count in those five, so both are in
# one log.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 201. charsiu computes MobileNet's failing shape correctly,
# so the hardware can do it and Mesa's configuration is what is wrong. This
# round takes Mesa's register stream off the board to say which register.
#
# ROUND 200: charsiu's M > 1 was never broken. The reading was.
#
#   M      surface reading            row major reading
#   1      64 of 64                   64 of 64          <- the control, agrees
#   2      128 of 128                 52 of 128
#   8      512 of 512                 209 of 512
#   56     3579 of 3584, 0 off by >1  1146 of 3584
#   224    14313 of 14336, 0 off by >1
#
# The output is [n/atom][m][n%atom], the mirror of the input surface, which at
# M = 1 collapses to exactly n. That is why row major had been right for 36
# rounds and only ever at one row. Nothing about the job changed: same stream,
# same packing, same buffers, only which byte is read. So the hardware was
# computing correctly the whole time and charsiu's own checker was wrong.
#
# AND THE LINE THAT MATTERS FOR MOBILENET:
#
#   M = 3136, K = 33, N = 64    200344 of 200704 byte exact, 51 off by >1
#
# That is pw33x64w56's exact arithmetic: 56 * 56 = 3136 rows, 33 input
# channels, 64 output channels. charsiu gets it to 0.025%. Mesa gets COMPUTED 0
# on the same shape. **The hardware can do it and Mesa is asking it wrongly.**
#
# So stop reasoning about Mesa's packing from the source and read its stream.
# ROCKET_DEBUG=dump_bos writes mesa-regcmd-000-NNN.bin, and this round hexdumps
# it into the log so it can be diffed offline against charsiu's stream for the
# same arithmetic, which is generated on the host with emit_job and needs no
# board at all.
#
# WHAT THE DIFF CAN AND CANNOT SAY. The two are not the same job: Mesa runs a
# 1x1 convolution over a 56 by 56 surface and charsiu a matmul with 3136 rows,
# so the geometry registers will differ and that means nothing. The registers
# that carry the CHANNEL COUNTS and the WEIGHT BYTE COUNTS are the comparable
# ones, and those are what round 196 changed in charsiu to fix exactly this.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   the dump appears and parses
#             the offline diff happens next round, against a stream already
#             generated here.
#   the dump is empty or missing
#             ROCKET_DEBUG did not reach the process, and the entry that runs
#             perch.py without it is the control that says whether the model
#             ran at all.
#   the dumped stream is byte identical to a working shape's
#             the fault is not in the register stream and the weight buffer
#             itself has to be dumped next.
#
# Entry 1 dumps a WORKING shape too, pw64x41w56, because a diff against a shape
# that computes on the same driver is worth more than a diff against charsiu,
# which is a different program entirely.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 200. The leftover constant is per channel, and the thing
# that made this round's priority change is that charsiu fails at every M above
# one while Mesa's failing shape is a 56 by 56 surface.
#
# ROUND 199, on the Mesa side.
#
#   affine fit, padding to ALIGN(ic,16):  a 0.9970  b median -31.41  corr 0.9907
#   b spread: min -108.28 max +117.16 std 46.47      VARIES per channel
#   lowest ch43 -108.3, highest ch59 +117.2
#
# So it is not one global constant. By the written rule that is the per channel
# A record. One more number from the same block, unexplained and worth keeping:
#
#   first 8 channel maxdiffs: [0, 0, 32, 32, 32, 0, 0, 9]
#
# Channels 2, 3 and 4 are off by EXACTLY 32.
#
# WHY THE PRIORITY MOVED. Round 198's control failure said charsiu is wrong at
# every M above 1, at a FLAT rate, 32.0% byte exact at K = 64 from M = 8 to
# M = 3136 and 38.7% at K = 33. Flat with size is what a wrong LAYOUT looks
# like, not what a wrong computation looks like. And Mesa's failing shape is a
# 56 by 56 surface while every shape that works reduces to one row. These are
# probably the same fault, and charsiu is the one with sparse probes on it.
#
# THE HYPOTHESIS, and it is a reading rather than a change to the hardware.
# charsiu's INPUT surface is [k/atom][m][k%atom]. If the output mirrors it, it
# is [n/atom][m][n%atom]. At M = 1 that expression collapses to exactly n, which
# is why a row major reading has been right all along AND ONLY AT M = 1.
#
# Nothing about the job moves this round: same register stream, same packing,
# same buffers. Only how the result is read, plus an output buffer sized
# ALIGN(N, atom) * M instead of M * N, because round 198's N = 40 came back
# "0 of 40 bytes written" and a short buffer is what that looks like.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   M > 1 becomes byte exact under the surface reading
#             the hardware was computing correctly the whole time and charsiu
#             was reading the answer out of the wrong bytes. Then the same
#             question goes to Mesa, whose output surface handling is where
#             MobileNet's 56 by 56 shapes would fail the same way.
#   M > 1 stays wrong
#             the layout is not the mirror, and the next step is a sparse probe
#             on M the way --map was a sparse probe on the weights.
#   M = 1 changes at all
#             the two readings are the same expression at M = 1, so anything
#             moving there means the buffer resize broke something and the
#             round is void. That is the control and it runs first.
#
# CHARSIU_OUT_ROWMAJOR forces the old reading, so both are in the same log.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 199. The padding unit is settled, one constant is left, and
# round 198's control failed in a way worth keeping.
#
# THE PADDING UNIT IS ALIGN(ic, FEATURE_ATOMIC_SIZE), three points:
#
#                        slope a   offset b   correlation
#   pad to 48 (atom 16)   0.9985     -31.46     +0.9963
#   pad to 64 (tile 32)   0.8110     -23.46     +0.8936
#   no pad    (33)        0.8875      +7.45     +0.8821
#
# 0.9963 is a straight line with one constant subtracted from it. Note that
# ALIGN(33, 32) is 64, not 48, and 64 is ALSO a multiple of 16 yet is worse. So
# it is not "more padding is better": the weight buffer has to be exactly as
# wide as task->input_channels, which rkt_task.c sets to ALIGN(MAX2(ic, 16),
# 16) and which the CNA's byte count is already derived from. Both halves now
# agree on one number.
#
# ROUND 198'S CONTROL FAILED, and it is a real finding rather than a wasted
# entry. M = 56 at K = 64 was supposed to be byte exact so that a failure at
# M = 56 K = 33 could not be blamed on M:
#
#   M=1    K=33      64 of 64          M=8    K=33   239 of 512
#   M=1    K=64      64 of 64          M=56   K=33  1384 of 3584
#   M=56   K=64    1146 of 3584        M=3136 K=33 77727 of 200704
#
# **charsiu is wrong at every M above 1.** The rate is FLAT: 38.7% exact at
# K=33 and 32.0% at K=64, unchanged from M=8 to M=3136, so it is not something
# that degrades with size. Every correctness run this project has done was at
# M = 1; M above 1 has only ever been measured for speed. Prefill is entirely
# M above 1, so this matters, but MobileNet is the priority and this is written
# down rather than chased today.
#
# By the written rule, entry 2 of round 198 says nothing about K at large M,
# because its control did not hold. It is not read that way here.
#
# ROUND 199'S QUESTION is the constant. With a slope of 0.9985 and a correlation
# of 0.9963 there is exactly one thing wrong with pw33x64w56, and the shape of
# the answer depends on whether it is the same constant on every channel:
#
#   b is the same on every channel (std below 0.5)
#             a single global term. The bias path, and the first place to look
#             is what the coefficient buffer's A record gets for a padded ic.
#   b varies per channel
#             the per-channel A record, and the lowest and highest channels
#             printed beside it say which ones and by how much.
#   b varies but in a few discrete groups
#             it tracks something quantised, and the group boundaries name it.
#
# perch.py now prints the spread, the standard deviation, a verdict, and the
# four lowest and four highest channels by name. Nothing else changed this
# round: Mesa, the kernel and charsiu are all byte identical to round 198, so
# every number that moves is the measurement getting sharper rather than the
# thing being measured moving.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 198. The padding DID work in Mesa and the channel counter
# hid it. Reading that counter instead of the fit was my mistake, and this
# round is about the constant that is left.
#
# ROUND 197, and the correction first. I read "14 of 64 with the padding and 18
# without" and called it no effect. The affine fit in the same block says
# otherwise:
#
#                     slope a   offset b   correlation
#   padding ON         0.9807     -31.23     +0.9338
#   padding OFF        0.4426      +9.38     +0.8656
#
# The slope went from 0.44 to 0.98 and the correlation from 0.87 to 0.93. The
# magnitude is right now; what is left is a CONSTANT offset of about -31. The
# channel counter only counts maxdiff <= 1, so a whole surface that is off by a
# constant scores zero either way. This project's own rule is that a matching
# channel is not a computed one; the converse is just as true, and an unmoved
# count is not an unchanged result.
#
# The controls all held: mn_conv0 32 of 32, conv2d-cal 128 of 128, pw64x41w56
# 41 of 41 every channel correct, and charsiu's K = 33 and 40 still 64 of 64.
#
# WHAT THE OFFSET IS NOT. The padded weights are written as biased zero, and the
# packer biases everything by 0x80, so a padded channel's (w - wz) is zero and
# multiplies to nothing whatever the padded input holds. The buffer size and the
# byte count the CNA is given both come out 48 * 64 = 3072. Neither of those can
# produce a constant, so the offset is somewhere else and guessing at it from
# here is what this round refuses to do.
#
# THE QUESTION, asked with the instrument that already works. charsiu computes
# K = 33 byte exact, but only at M = 1. Mesa's failing shape is the same 33
# input channels with a 56 by 56 surface. So make charsiu carry the surface.
#
#   charsiu at M = 56 and M = 3136 with K = 33 is byte exact
#             the geometry is not the problem and Mesa's remaining fault is in
#             its configuration, which can then be diffed register by register
#             against a stream that is known to compute at this shape.
#   charsiu loses byte exactness as M grows
#             it is the surface and not the channel count, and the same is
#             probably true of every other unexplained Mesa shape.
#   charsiu is exact at M = 56 but not at 3136
#             there is a size limit in between, and where it is matters more
#             than what it is.
#
# THE CONTROL: M = 1 at K = 33 and K = 64 must still be 64 of 64, and M = 56 at
# K = 64 too, so a failure at M = 56 K = 33 cannot be blamed on M alone.
#
# MESA SIDE, one variable at a time. The padding rounds to FEATURE_ATOMIC_SIZE,
# which is 16, because that is where charsiu's boundary was measured. The
# pointwise weight TILE is 32. If rounding to 32 changes the offset, then the
# padding unit is the tile and not the atom, and if it does not, that is one
# more thing the offset is not.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 197. charsiu's fix works, so the same change goes into Mesa
# and MobileNet is what it is being asked about.
#
# ROUND 196 ON charsiu, control passing first (64, 512x1024 and 96 all still
# byte exact):
#
#   K            31    33    40    63    65
#   fixed      64/64 64/64 64/64 64/64 64/64
#   NO_KALIGN  30/64 37/64 25/64 21/64 43/64
#
# Five for five, and the impulse at 33, 40 and 65 is 64 of 64 as well. Laying
# the weight groups out against K rounded up to the feature atom, and telling
# the CNA that K, is the whole of it.
#
# MESA HAS THE SAME BUG, and had it in a more obvious form once the atom was
# the thing to look at:
#
#   the INPUT side pads. rkt_task.c sets task->input_channels to
#   ALIGN(MAX2(ic, FEATURE_ATOMIC_SIZE), FEATURE_ATOMIC_SIZE), and the CNA's
#   weight byte count in rkt_regcmd.c is computed FROM THAT.
#
#   the WEIGHT side did not. rkt_coefs.c sized the pointwise buffer as
#   input_channels_real * ALIGN(oc, 2) and cut its ic tiles short with MIN2.
#
# So the buffer was SHORTER than the byte count the CNA had been given, and
# every ic group after the first started somewhere the hardware was not reading.
# That is a stronger statement than charsiu's: here the two numbers can be
# compared directly in the source.
#
# The fix is rkt_pw_ic_padded(), one line either side, with the padded weights
# left at the zero point where they meet the padded inputs' zero point.
# ROCKET_PW_NO_ICPAD restores the old form.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   pw33x64w56 goes from 16 of 64 to 64 of 64
#             the atom was the whole story on both stacks, and MobileNet's last
#             open operation is closed.
#   it improves but is not exact
#             the padding is necessary and not sufficient, and the remainder is
#             a second fault at the same shape.
#   it does not move
#             Mesa's fault is not charsiu's, despite the same source level
#             mistake, and entry 3's before-and-after says so in one log.
#   mn_conv0, conv2d-cal or pw64x41w56 stops being correct
#             the padding broke a shape that already worked. Those three are
#             the control and they run FIRST.
#
# pw64x41w56 has 64 input channels and is already right, so it is the case the
# fix must not touch. conv2d-cal has 128. mn_conv0 is a firstconv and takes a
# different path entirely.
#
# ENTRY 3 runs the failing shape with ROCKET_PW_NO_ICPAD, so before and after
# are in the same log rather than across two rounds. Round 192 was compared
# across rounds and that is not a control.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 196. The line is at 16, not 32, and the fix follows from
# that directly.
#
# ROUND 195'S SWEEP, int8, N = 64, control passing (K=64 was 64 of 64 and
# K=512 N=1024 unchanged):
#
#   K    16   31   32   33   40   48   63   64   65   96
#        64   28   63   38   25   64   21   64   39   64      of 64 byte exact
#
# BYTE EXACT EXACTLY WHEN K IS A MULTIPLE OF 16. 16, 32, 48, 64 and 96 are
# right; 31, 33, 40, 63 and 65 are wrong. **40 is a multiple of 8 and is wrong**,
# so the boundary is 16 and not 8, and 48 is right so it is not 32 either. The
# impulse runs say the same thing from the other side: K=32 was 64 of 64, K=33
# was 33 of 64, K=40 was 42 of 64.
#
# 16 IS THE int8 FEATURE ATOM, which every one of those lines printed beside
# itself as "feature atom 16".
#
# THAT MOVES THE SUSPECT. This has been written down since round 176 as
# "input channels above 32", and Mesa's pw64x41w56 has SIXTY FOUR input channels
# and is right while pw33x64w56 has thirty three and is wrong. It was never
# about 32. It is the feature atom, and the two sides of the packing disagreed
# about it:
#
#   the INPUT packer pads. charsiu_pack_input memsets the whole surface to the
#   zero point before writing, so the tail of the last atom contributes nothing.
#
#   the WEIGHT packer cut short. kgsz = MIN2(mm->k - kgi*kg, kg) ended the last
#   k group at the real K, so every group after the first began somewhere the
#   hardware was not reading from.
#
# Mesa does the same thing, [oc/32][ic/32][oc%32][ic%32] with MIN2 edges, and
# that cut-short rule was read OFFLINE from vendor blobs at 48 by 40. It has
# never been confirmed on hardware at a K that is not a multiple of the atom.
#
# THE FIX: lay the weight groups out against K rounded up to the feature atom,
# and tell the CNA that K. The padded weights stay at the weight zero point and
# meet the padded inputs' zero point, so they multiply to nothing. Verified
# offline before flashing:
#
#   K=33 and K=40 now emit 0x107c = 0x2f, the same as K=48
#   at K = 16, 32, 48, 64, 96 and 512 the whole stream is byte identical with
#   the fix on and off, so nothing that already worked can move
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   31, 33, 40, 63, 65 come back 64 of 64
#             the atom is the whole story, and the same change goes into Mesa,
#             where it should fix pw33x64w56 and MobileNet with it.
#   some of them fix and some do not
#             read WHICH. 33 and 65 are one past an atom, 31 and 63 are one
#             short of one, and 40 is halfway; those three cases failing
#             differently would say the rule is not a plain round up.
#   none of them move
#             padding the weights is not what the hardware wanted and the input
#             side is where to look next, since that is the half that was
#             already padding.
#   K = 64 or K = 512 stops being byte exact
#             the fix broke what worked. That is the control, and it runs first.
#
# CHARSIU_NO_KALIGN restores the old behaviour and entry 3 runs the failing K
# under it, so the before and after sit in the same log rather than in two
# rounds.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 195. The w4a16 output is solved, so this round turns to
# MobileNet, and it asks charsiu the question Mesa cannot ask itself.
#
# ROUND 194 CLOSED THE w4a16 OUTPUT, exactly:
#
#   out = ((int16)fp16bits(w) * (int16)fp16bits(a)) >> 16      18 of 18 exact
#
# The hardware multiplies the two operands' fp16 BIT PATTERNS as signed 16 bit
# integers and shifts right 16. All four non powers of two landed on that with
# zero error against the true logarithm reading, which the script had predicted
# to 20 counts apart before the run, and both negative nibbles fit too. That is
# charsiu's next problem and it is well posed now, so MobileNet moves ahead of
# it: the community is waiting on the driver, not on the LLM runtime.
#
# WHAT MOBILENET IS ACTUALLY STUCK ON. Mesa's own regressions in the same log:
#
#   mn_conv0        32 of 32     every channel correct
#   conv2d-cal     128 of 128    every channel correct
#   pw64x41w56      41 of 41     every channel correct
#   pw33x64w56      13 of 64     51 channels wrong, maxdiff 255, COMPUTED 0
#   mobilenet     1000 of 1001   but COMPUTED 0, npu distinct 9: all trivial
#
# pw64x41w56 has SIXTY FOUR input channels and is right. pw33x64w56 has
# THIRTY THREE and is wrong. So it is not "ic above 32", which is how this has
# been written down since round 176. It is ic not being a multiple of 32.
#
# WHAT BOTH STACKS ASSUME, and it is the same assumption in both. Mesa packs a
# pointwise as [oc/32][ic/32][oc%32][ic%32] with the edge tiles CUT SHORT rather
# than padded, MIN2(ic_n - ig*32, 32). charsiu's int8 packer does exactly the
# same thing, kgsz = MIN2(mm->k - kgi*kg, kg). The cut-short rule was read
# OFFLINE from vendor blobs at 48 by 40; it has never been confirmed on hardware
# at a K that is not a multiple of 32.
#
# AND charsiu CAN ASK. Its int8 matmul is byte exact, but only ever at K = 64
# and K = 512, both multiples of 32. Its --map probe is a known good instrument
# on int8: 512 of 512 probes light exactly one channel with n = byte / 32 and
# k = byte % 32, matching the packer. A matmul IS a 1x1 convolution, so K is ic.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   K = 33 comes back byte exact
#             the cut-short rule is right and MobileNet's fault is elsewhere,
#             in the registers or the CBUF rather than the weight layout. That
#             would be a real result: it retires the layout as a suspect.
#   K = 33 is not byte exact
#             the same assumption is wrong in both stacks, and --map at K = 33
#             reads what the hardware actually fetches, one live byte at a time,
#             with no theory in the way.
#   K = 32 and K = 64 are byte exact and 31, 33, 40, 65 are not
#             it is alignment and nothing else.
#   everything is byte exact
#             charsiu's packing differs from Mesa's somewhere that matters, and
#             the diff between the two is the next thing to read.
#
# THE CONTROL: K = 64 N = 64 must come back 64 of 64 byte exact, as it has since
# round 164, and K = 512 N = 1024 must be unchanged. If either moves, the
# int8 path changed underneath this comparison and nothing here can be read.
#
# --map runs at K = 33 whatever the matmul says, because a map is worth having
# either way: it is the only thing in this tree that reads a layout instead of
# inferring one from whether the answer came out right.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 194. The output is logarithmic in its inputs, and this round
# writes down the numbers each explanation predicts before the board is asked.
#
# ROUND 193, two clean curves, eleven runs, every all zero control passing.
#
#   activation, nibble fixed at 7        nibble, activation fixed at 8.0
#     1.0 ->  4260                         1 ->  4320       8 (signed -8) -> -4032
#     2.0 ->  4544   +284                  2 ->  4608 +288 15 (signed -1) -> -4896
#     4.0 ->  4828   +284                  3 ->  4752
#     8.0 ->  5112   +284                  4 ->  4896 +288
#                                          7 ->  5112
#
# DOUBLING AN INPUT ADDS A CONSTANT. Three times +284 on the activation, twice
# +288 on the weight, exact each time. So the output is linear in log2 of its
# inputs and not in the inputs, which is why nibble 7 to 3 gave 0.930 instead of
# 3/7 and why no line fits two points of it.
#
# The nibble curve says more than that. The differences divided by 288 are
# 0, 1, 1.5, 2, 2.75 at w = 1, 2, 3, 4, 7, and that is the cheap log2 you get
# from taking the exponent and adding the mantissa: 3 = 2^1 * 1.5 gives 1 + 0.5,
# 7 = 2^2 * 1.75 gives 2 + 0.75. Five points, no exceptions.
#
# THE WEDGE FIX HELD. int8 first exit 0, and exit 0 again after five w4a16 jobs.
# The last int8 failed and was declared in advance to say nothing, since entry
# 4's second half wedges on purpose.
#
# TWO EXPLANATIONS FIT EVERYTHING SO FAR, and they separate on values that are
# not powers of two. Written before the run, with the numbers:
#
#   A  the activation is read as an INTEGER, its fp16 bit pattern
#        out = 0.277344 * bits(a)
#   B  the output is a true logarithm
#        out = 4260 + 284 * log2(a)
#
#        a      fp16 bits    A says    B says    apart
#      1.5          15872      4402      4426       24
#      3.0          16896      4686      4710       24
#      5.0          17664      4899      4919       20
#      6.0          17920      4970      4994       24
#     16.0          19456      5396      5396        0
#     32.0          20480      5680      5680        0
#
#   C  the weight goes through the same cheap log2, exponent plus mantissa
#        out = 4320 + 288 * (e + m)
#   D  the weight goes through a true log2
#        out = 4320 + 288 * log2(w)
#
#        w      C says    D says    apart
#        5        4968      4989       21
#        6        5040      5064       24
#
# THE DECISION RULE.
#
#   every non power of two lands within 5 of A
#             the activation reaches the hardware as the integer 0x3C00 and so
#             on, which means charsiu's fp16 packing is being read as an int and
#             the whole activation format question reopens.
#   every one lands within 5 of B
#             the output really is a logarithm of the product, and the next
#             question is what stage computes it.
#   they land on neither
#             both explanations are out and the curves need a third.
#   they land on A for some and B for others
#             no conclusion, and the run says the probe is not isolating one
#             thing.
#
#   16.0 and 32.0 are NOT a discrimination, both explanations give 5396 and
#   5680. They are an extrapolation check: if either misses, the linearity
#   itself stops holding outside the range it was measured in, and that matters
#   more than which of A or B is right.
#
#   w = 5 and 6 decide C against D on the same principle.
#
# THE CONTROL: activation 8.0 at nibble 7 is re-run and must come back 5112,
# the value it has given in rounds 185 through 193. If it moves, the probe
# changed underneath the comparison and nothing here can be read.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 193. The wedge is fixed and the fix survived the control
# that could have taken it out, so this round goes back to the actual question:
# what function of the weight and the activation the output is.
#
# ROUND 192, all three results.
#
#   THE WEDGE IS GONE. One default w4a16 job then int8: exit 0. Four more: exit
#   0. That action timed out in every round from 185 to 191.
#
#   THE CONTROL THAT COULD HAVE UNDONE THE FIX PASSED, and in the strongest
#   form available: the byte map with the RDMA group OFF and the byte map with
#   it FORCED ON are identical line for line, all 40 rows, same histogram.
#   "That group changes nothing about the output" is now one direct comparison
#   rather than three separate old measurements.
#
#   AND IT WAS COSTING ONE PROBE. K=16 N=64 came back 159 probes at 2 non zero
#   output bytes and 1 at 16 or more, twice, in rounds 190 and 191, both with
#   the group on. With the group off it is 160 at 2 and no outlier. That is a
#   cross round comparison, not a control, so entry 4 below runs both halves in
#   the same log.
#
# WHAT IS ACTUALLY OPEN. The output is not weight times activation:
#
#   nibble  7 (signed  +7), activation 8.0   ->  +5112
#   nibble 15 (signed  -1), activation 8.0   ->  -4896
#   nibble  0                                ->  output all zero
#
# Fit a line through those two: out = 1251 * w - 3645, which predicts -3645 at
# w = 0 where the measurement is 0. Fit out = C * w and the two points give
# C = 730.3 and C = 4896. Neither works, so out is not an affine function of the
# nibble either, and this has TWO points and a rule against fitting to part of a
# map.
#
# So take more points, in ONE probe rather than across rounds. Round 184's
# 0.930 ratio came from a different probe with a different activation and
# cannot be laid beside these.
#
#   A. activation fixed at 8.0, nibble over 1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 8, 15
#   B. nibble fixed at 7, activation over 1.0, 2.0, 4.0, 8.0
#
# Step 256, so each run is 8 probes of which 4 land in live bytes. Enough to
# read the value; the layout is already mapped and is not what is being asked.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   A is proportional to the SIGNED nibble
#             the MAC is right and everything left is scaling.
#   A is proportional to the UNSIGNED nibble
#             the weights are not being read as signed, which contradicts
#             nibble 15 coming back negative and would need that explained.
#   A is affine with a non zero intercept
#             something is being added that the coefficient buffer should be
#             setting and is not, and 0x40ac is where to look.
#   A is neither
#             the nibble is not entering the arithmetic as a number, and the
#             next question is the packing rather than the output stage.
#
#   B is proportional to the activation
#             the activation is entering as a number.
#   B is flat
#             the activation is not in the MAC at all and every reading that
#             assumed it was comes back out.
#   B moves but not proportionally
#             the activation is not being read as fp16, and its own format is
#             the next question.
#
# The all zero control runs first in every one of the eleven runs, as always.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 192. The register is named, so the fix goes in the default
# stream, and this round is the fix plus the control that can undo it.
#
# ROUND 191, six masks, every row valid: baseline recovered AND the w4a16 job
# confirmed to have written, on all six.
#
#   0xf   all four        int8 TIMED OUT      the repro, run first
#   0     none            int8 byte exact     the control
#   1     0x501c only     int8 byte exact
#   2     0x5034 only     int8 TIMED OUT
#   4     0x5040 only     int8 byte exact
#   8     0x5044 only     int8 TIMED OUT
#
# Two registers wedge the next job on their own. A LEAD AND NOT AN EXPLANATION:
# both differ from int8 in bit 30 and in opposite directions, int8 being
# (0x5034 clear, 0x5044 set) and w4a16 (0x5034 set, 0x5044 clear), so touching
# one alone makes both set or both clear. But mask 0xf sets them the way the
# vendor's own stream does and wedges anyway, so bit 30 does not close it.
#
# THE FIX. The RDMA coefficient fetch group is out of the default stream. It
# buys nothing measurable: rounds 182 and 183 put two knobs on the coefficient
# buffer and got four byte identical runs with the group ON, and round 186's run
# without it produced element 0 = -3692, the same value as with it. A group that
# alters no result and wedges the next job does not ship. It stays reachable one
# bit per register through CHARSIU_W4_RDMA_MASK, because when the coefficient
# surface does have to be fetched, this is where that starts.
#
# Checked offline: the default stream is now byte identical to mask 0, differs
# from mask 0xf in exactly those four registers, and int8's stream is unchanged
# from round 191's.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   entry 2, one w4a16 job then int8, comes back byte exact
#             the wedge is gone from the default path.
#   entry 2 still times out
#             the fix is wrong and the two registers were not the whole of it.
#
#   THE CONTROL THAT CAN UNDO THE FIX is entry 3. The byte map at K=64 N=64 has
#   to come back IDENTICAL to round 191's: 640 weight bytes lighting something,
#   1408 lighting nothing, output byte = (weight byte / 8) * 4, value 5112, and
#   the same histogram. If ANY of that moves, then this group did affect the
#   output after all, three separate measurements were wrong, and the fix has to
#   come back out. That is the branch this round is really being run for.
#
#   entry 4 runs the same map with the group forced back ON via the mask. It
#   must match entry 3. Two maps, one with and one without, is what turns "it
#   changes nothing" from three old measurements into one direct comparison.
#
# AND THE OUTLIER GETS NAMED. K=16 N=64 came back twice with 159 probes at 2 non
# zero output bytes and exactly one at 16 or more, the same numbers both times,
# so it is a feature and not noise. --bmap now prints any probe over 4 whatever
# the line limit says, so the weight byte that does it will be in the log.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 191. Round 190 named the group. The group is four registers,
# so name the register.
#
# ROUND 190'S BISECTION, and every clause of the rule mattered:
#
#   the full port   baseline OK on attempt 3   1 job wrote   int8 exit 1
#   NO_DPU          baseline OK on attempt 2   0 jobs wrote  VOID
#   NO_RDMA         baseline OK on attempt 1   1 job wrote   int8 exit 0
#   both out        baseline OK on attempt 1   1 job wrote   int8 exit 0
#
# So the RDMA coefficient fetch group is what leaves the NPU unable to start the
# next job. NO_DPU's row was thrown out by the written rule rather than read as
# a pass, which is the trap round 186 fell into with the same configuration. And
# the full port's baseline needed THREE attempts, so 186's bet that one Mesa
# model would put the device back was never going to hold.
#
# THAT GROUP CHANGES NOTHING ABOUT THE OUTPUT. Round 182 put two knobs on the
# coefficient buffer and got four byte identical runs, round 183 ported this
# fetch and they stayed identical, and round 186's NO_RDMA run produced element
# 0 = -3692, the same as the full port. Four registers that alter no result and
# wedge the next job are pure liability, and which one of them it is, is worth
# exactly one round.
#
#   bit 0  0x501c = 0          int8 writes 0x00000710
#   bit 1  0x5034 = 4000004c   int8 writes 0x00000041
#   bit 2  0x5040 = rows       int8 writes 0
#   bit 3  0x5044 = 000280a1   int8 writes 0x40000010
#
# Verified offline that each bit moves exactly one register and one line of the
# stream, that mask 0xf is the default stream and mask 0 is NO_RDMA, and that
# int8's own stream is untouched by the mask at any value.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN. A row counts only if its baseline
# came back AND its w4a16 job wrote, same as last round.
#
#   mask 0xf does not wedge      the repro is gone and the whole round is void.
#                                It is the first row for that reason.
#   mask 0 wedges                that contradicts round 190 and the round is
#                                void as well.
#   exactly one single bit row wedges
#                                that register is it.
#   several single bit rows wedge
#                                each of them does it on its own.
#   no single bit row wedges
#                                it takes a combination, and the next round is
#                                the six pairs rather than another guess.
#
# ALSO RE-RUN: the K=16 N=64 byte map. Round 190 got 159 probes at 2 non zero
# output bytes and ONE at 16 or more, and that single outlier is what made
# round 189's table at this geometry unreadable. Whether it repeats says whether
# it is a real feature of the layout or a one off.
#
# The rest of the geometry is settled and both formulas held at four geometries
# with every control passing:
#
#   written    = 2N + 32                   N=64 -> 160, N=32 -> 96
#   live bytes = K * (N + 16) / 8          16, 32, 64, 128 at N=64, and 64x32
#
# 64x32 came out 384 here against 383 last round, so that off by one was noise.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 190. One w4a16 job is enough to wedge the NPU, so the
# bisection that failed twice can finally be run against a one job repro.
#
# ROUND 189, BOTH ANSWERS.
#
# THE ELEMENT IS FOUR BYTES, and it was the fallback branch of the written rule
# that said so rather than the main one:
#
#   nibble 7   ->  2 non zero output bytes, at all four geometries
#   nibble 15  ->  4 non zero output bytes
#
# 7 gives 5112 = 0x000013F8, whose top half IS zero; 15 gives -4896 =
# 0xFFFFECE0, which has no zero byte in it. Only a four byte element whose high
# bytes may be zero explains both histograms, which is exactly the case the rule
# named in advance and put nibble 15 in the run for. Round 185's reading stands
# and the README does not come out.
#
#   K=64 N=64   output byte = (weight byte / 8) * 4
#               16 weight bytes per output, two runs of 8 that are 256 apart
#               40 output positions, written 160 = 40 * 4
#   K=64 N=16   16 output positions, stride 4, written 64
#               element count = N/2 + 8 holds at N = 16, 32 and 64
#
# ONE ROW IS NOT CLEAN and no conclusion is drawn from it: K=16 N=64 came back
# with 8 or 9 weight bytes per output, first values of 0 and 1 sitting next to
# each other, and only 20 output positions against K=64's 40. It is re-run here.
#
# A SINGLE w4a16 JOB WEDGES IT.
#
#   N = 0 (bare charsiu_matmul)   exit 0        the control passed
#   --pre 1, 1 job that wrote     int8 exit 1
#   --pre 2, 4, 8                 int8 exit 1, and those jobs no longer wrote
#   --pre 16                      int8 exit 0
#
# The first row is the answer and the rest are downstream of it, which is what
# the script said to read. A one job repro is what makes a bisection cheap.
#
# ROUND 190'S QUESTION, and its rule, written before the run.
#
# Four configurations, one w4a16 job each, judged by charsiu_matmul in its own
# process. Before each one, charsiu_matmul is run up to four times as BOTH the
# baseline and the recovery, because round 186 bet its bisection on a single
# Mesa model recovering the device and that model was itself broken.
#
#   the baseline never comes back in four tries
#             that configuration's verdict is VOID. Not a pass.
#
#   --pre reports 0 jobs wrote
#             that configuration's verdict is VOID TOO, and this is the exact
#             trap round 186 fell into: its NO_DPU run wrote not one byte, so
#             "int8 was fine afterwards" only meant no w4a16 job had run.
#
#   full wedges, NO_DPU does not, and its job wrote
#             the DPU output stage group does it.
#   full wedges, NO_RDMA does not, and its job wrote
#             the RDMA coefficient fetch group does it.
#   neither wedges and both jobs wrote
#             it takes the two groups together.
#   both wedge
#             each group does it alone, or it is 0x100c, the int4 weight format
#             itself, which is what the "both out" row is there to ask: that row
#             is the round 180 stream with only the int4 format left.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 189. Two of round 188's numbers are formulas now; the third
# quantity it printed was one this file invented, and it is taken back out.
#
# WHAT SIX GEOMETRIES SAID, every all zero control passing:
#
#   K=64  N=64   buffer 2048   written 160   live 640
#   K=16  N=64          512    written 160   live 160
#   K=32  N=64         1024    written 160   live 320
#   K=64  N=32         1024    written  96   live 383
#   K=64  N=16          512    written  64   live 256
#   K=128 N=64 step 2  4096    written 160   live 640, so 1280 at step 1
#
#   written = 2N + 32, at three values of N, and it does NOT depend on K
#   live bytes = 10K at N = 64, at K = 16, 32, 64 and 128
#   live bytes = K * (N + 16) / 8 over all six, with N = 32 one byte short
#
# **k >= 16 IS FETCHED.** That has been written as "no data at all" since round
# 175. The live count is linear in K and K = 128 does not saturate it.
#
# AND THE THING THIS FILE INVENTED. --bmap reported a "slot", which was the
# first non zero output byte DIVIDED BY FOUR, and four was an assumption. The
# six tables then disagreed with each other: at K = 16 only even slots ever
# appeared and each held 8 bytes, and at K = 32 slot 0's first weight byte came
# out as 257 rather than 0. A quantity that is being MEASURED cannot also be an
# input to the measurement, so the division is gone and --bmap now reports the
# output byte itself, how many output bytes came back non zero, and a histogram
# of that count.
#
# THE HISTOGRAM IS THE POINT. Every probe writes exactly one value, so the
# number of non zero output bytes is how wide that value is. Round 186's sweep
# printed "bytes 0..1" every single time and this project still wrote down four,
# because the hex dump beside it was aligned to four. written = 2 * (N + 16) is
# the other half of the same doubt.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   the histogram piles up at 2
#             the element is two bytes and the "4 byte integer accumulator" in
#             the README comes out, along with round 185's reading of it.
#   the histogram piles up at 4
#             it is four, and written = 2(N+16) needs its own explanation.
#   the histogram is spread
#             a value's own bytes can be zero and this probe cannot settle the
#             width on its own; the next step is a nibble chosen to make every
#             byte of the result non zero.
#
# AND THE WEDGE PROBE IS REPLACED RATHER THAN FIXED. --wedge hand built an int8
# job and ran it in the same process. Its N = 0 control failed in round 187 and
# failed AGAIN in round 188 after the five pattern probes were taken out of the
# way, so zero w4a16 jobs and a hand built int8 job still came back holding the
# sentinel. The instrument was broken, not the thing measured. --pre N submits
# N w4a16 jobs and exits, and the int8 job that judges it is charsiu_matmul in
# its own process, which is the path that has been byte exact since round 164
# and has not been touched since.
#
# That is two processes again, which is what round 186 lost its bisection to.
# It is paid differently here: nothing has to RECOVER between the two, the
# counts go from small to large so the first failure is the answer, and the
# N = 0 row is charsiu_matmul alone, which entry 1 has already shown passing in
# the same log.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 188. The byte to slot map is read; now make the geometry
# move and let the shape of the map say the formula.
#
# ROUND 187'S --bmap, control passed, and NO byte lit more than one slot:
#
#   byte b feeds output slot b / 8      eight consecutive bytes per slot
#   each slot fed by 16 bytes           two runs of eight, 256 apart
#   640 bytes lit something             1408 lit nothing, and the whole
#                                       second half of the buffer is dark
#   value 5112 everywhere, write extent 160 bytes = 40 slots
#
# By 64 byte blocks the live ones are 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 11, 12, 14, 15 and the
# dead ones 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 13, with each slot group fed by two blocks four
# apart: (0,4) (2,6) (8,12) (10,14) (11,15). Ten blocks alternate and then the
# pattern breaks twice. NO RULE IS WRITTEN FROM THAT. A map read at ONE geometry
# cannot separate what depends on K from what depends on N, and K = 64 with an
# int4 k-group of 16 is exactly the case where the two get confused.
#
# So move the geometry. 40 slots where N = 64, and 640 live bytes where N * 8 is
# 512, are both numbers that have to come from somewhere, and changing K and N
# separately is what says which.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   the slot count tracks N and ignores K
#             a slot is an output channel, and the 40 at N = 64 is something
#             else that has to be explained on its own.
#   the slot count moves with K
#             a slot is not an output channel, and every reading in this tree
#             that called it one has to be redone.
#   the write extent is always 4 * the slot count
#             the element really is four bytes, at every geometry rather than
#             at the one it was read at.
#   K = 16 lights the WHOLE buffer and K = 128 lights no more than K = 64
#             only k < 16 is ever fetched, and the dead bands at K = 64 are the
#             k >= 16 half that has been missing since round 175.
#   K = 128 lights proportionally more
#             k >= 16 IS fetched and the K = 64 pattern is a real layout that
#             has simply not been read yet.
#
# The control is the same at every geometry and runs first at each: an all zero
# weight buffer must light nothing.
#
# AND --wedge GETS THE CLEAN START IT NEEDED. Round 187 asked how many w4a16
# jobs it takes before the NPU cannot start an int8 one, and its N = 0 row had
# FIVE w4a16 jobs behind it, because the five pattern probes in main() run
# before every sub command and the weight dtype there is int4. Its control
# caught that and the table was thrown away. Sub commands now skip that loop.
#
# TWO THINGS ALREADY KNOWN ABOUT THE WEDGE, neither needing a board:
#
#   w4a16 jobs do NOT wedge each other. --bmap ran 2049 of them back to back
#   with no failure at all. Only an int8 job afterwards cannot start, so this is
#   about the SWITCH rather than about the NPU dying.
#
#   it is not an uncovered register. int4's stream and int8's stream write the
#   SAME 143 registers, checked offline; 18 of them differ in value and int8
#   writes all 18. So nothing the w4a16 job set is left unwritten by the int8
#   job that follows it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 187. Round 186's map is clean; its bisection failed by its
# own written rule, and both of this round's probes exist because of that.
#
# THE MAP, all 64 channels, control passed (no live nibble, written region all
# zero):
#
#   lit    0-7   16-23   32-39   48-63     40 channels, slots 0..39, no overlap
#   dark   8-15  24-31   40-47             24 channels light nothing
#   value  0x000013f8 = 5112 everywhere, independent of the channel
#   extent 160 bytes = 40 slots, where N = 64
#
# NO RULE IS WRITTEN DOWN FROM THAT, because the word "channel" in it is an
# assumption. The probe put its live nibble at row = (n/32)*512 + (n%32)*8, and
# that formula came from --map in rounds 176 to 179, when the output still went
# through the int8 requant and was read as BYTES. The w4a16 path writes 4 byte
# integers into a 160 byte region. The two paths are not known to agree, and
# three evenly spaced dead bands are exactly what a wrong row formula looks
# like. --bmap drops the formula and walks the weight buffer a byte at a time.
#
# THE BISECTION FAILED, and the check that caught it was written before the run.
#
#   entry 4's Mesa recovery was itself broken, conv2d-cal 0 of 128, EMPTY conv,
#   npu distinct 1. The header said in that case entries 5 and 7 say nothing.
#   entry 6's Mesa run was fine, 128 of 128, so the recovery is real but its
#   timing is not controlled.
#
#   entry 7, NO_DPU, wrote NOT ONE BYTE: 256 of 256 still held the sentinel. So
#   "int8 was fine afterwards" only means no w4a16 job ever ran. Entry 8 wrote
#   64 bytes of 0x7f807f80, which is the int8 rail, so that was not w4a16
#   either. Only entry 5, NO_RDMA, actually ran one: 160 bytes written and
#   element 0 = -3692, identical to the full port, which incidentally says the
#   RDMA group changes nothing about the output and matches rounds 182 and 183.
#
#   and the COUNT was never controlled. The failing case had 65 w4a16 jobs
#   behind it, from --osweep. The passing ones had one.
#
# SO --wedge DOES IT IN ONE PROCESS. Same fd, no Mesa in between, nothing
# shared, and the count is the only thing that moves: N w4a16 jobs, then one
# int8 job, then report whether the int8 job completed. N runs 0, 1, 2, 4, 8,
# 16, 32, 64 and the totals ACCUMULATE, so the running total is printed beside
# N and a threshold is in terms of the total.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   N = 0 fails            the int8 job is broken on its own and this has
#                          nothing to do with w4a16. The control that can fail.
#   first failure at a row the threshold is between that row's total and the
#                          previous row's.
#   every row passes       a plain w4a16 job wedges nothing, and whatever round
#                          185 hit is in what --osweep does differently rather
#                          than in the count.
#
# AND --bmap READS THE LAYOUT WITHOUT THE FORMULA. One live nibble in one byte,
# every byte of the 2048, activation a single constant, output slot recorded,
# then a summary per slot of how many bytes feed it and over what span.
#
#   contiguous run of bytes per slot   the run is the row size and the gap
#                                      between runs is the row stride.
#   scattered bytes per slot           the layout is interleaved, and the per
#                                      slot min and max say over what span.
#   a large part of the buffer dark    the fetch is shorter than the buffer,
#                                      and this measures by how much.
#
# Its control is the all zero buffer, run FIRST, which must light nothing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 186. Two results from round 185, and the second one is a
# defect in the stream this project wrote.
#
# THE OUTPUT ELEMENT IS FOUR BYTES, read rather than fitted. One live nibble in
# the whole weight buffer lights exactly four bytes, aligned to four, and the
# value is a signed integer: nibble 7 gave 0x000013f8 = +5112 and nibble 15,
# which is -1 as a signed nibble, gave 0xffffece0 = -4896. The sign follows the
# signed nibble. So round 181's "the output is a float" is dead: the registers
# only ever said the stage does not requantise, and an un-requantised output is
# the accumulator.
#
# THE CONTROL PASSED AGAIN. No live nibble anywhere and the written region came
# back all zero.
#
# BUT NINE CHANNELS DID NOT AGREE, so no rule is being written down yet.
#
#   channel   0 1 2 3 4 5   8   16  32  63
#   byte      0 4 8 12 16 20  none 32  64  156
#
# 0 to 5 land at 4n. 16 lands at 32 and 32 at 64, which is 2n. 63 lands at 156,
# which is neither. And channel 8 lights NOTHING. Nine points cannot carry a
# rule and fitting one to part of a map is exactly what cost rounds 171 and
# 173, so round 186 sweeps ALL 64 and prints the table.
#
# The other unexplained number is the extent: the hardware writes 160 bytes,
# which is 40 slots of 4, where 64 channels want 64. Nothing is assumed about
# that either; the sweep prints the extent on every row.
#
# THE SECOND RESULT: A w4a16 JOB LEAVES THE NPU UNABLE TO START THE NEXT ONE.
# This was as clean as this project gets. Same boot, same binary, same register
# stream, same shape:
#
#   entry 1, int8 FIRST          1023 of 1024 byte exact, exit 0
#   entry 7, int8 LAST           NPU job timed out, output still holding the
#                                0xa5 sentinel, exit 1, rk_iommu "Error during
#                                raw reset" beside it
#
# Only what ran in between differs. It is not the stream: int8's stream at both
# shapes is byte identical before and after the w4a16 port, checked offline
# against e46a56d. Mesa's own regressions ran fine afterwards, so the driver
# recovers and nothing is permanently broken.
#
# ROUND 186 SPLITS THE PORT AND ASKS WHICH HALF. It went in as two groups from
# two separate vendor streams, so each comes back out on its own:
#
#   CHARSIU_W4_NO_DPU    0x4010 0x4030 0x4038 0x4044 0x4050 0x40b0 0x40b4
#   CHARSIU_W4_NO_RDMA   0x501c 0x5034 0x5040 0x5044
#
# Verified offline that each switch touches only its own group and that int8's
# stream is untouched by both.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   int8 passes after NO_RDMA and fails after NO_DPU
#             the RDMA coefficient fetch group is what wedges the next job.
#   int8 passes after NO_DPU and fails after NO_RDMA
#             the DPU output stage group is.
#   int8 fails after both
#             either group does it on its own, or it is 0x100c, the int4 weight
#             format itself, which is in neither group and cannot be removed
#             without the run stopping being w4a16.
#   int8 passes after both
#             it takes the two groups together, which is a real answer too.
#
# THE ASSUMPTION THIS RUN RESTS ON, and it is checked rather than assumed: that
# a Mesa model in between puts the NPU back. Entries 4 and 6 are that check. If
# either of those Mesa runs is itself broken, the recovery does not happen and
# entries 5 and 7 say nothing at all.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-16 round 185. The output element is not two bytes wide, and every
# w4a16 number this file has printed was read at the wrong width.
#
# ROUND 184'S RESULT, and it settled two of its three questions.
#
#   THE CONTROL PASSED. With no live nibble anywhere the output came back all
#   0x0000, one distinct value, TOO FLAT fired. So the weight buffer IS read
#   and the output is not invented. The worst case in the handoff is gone.
#
#   THE NIBBLE RULE FIRED, and it was the independent and harder of the two.
#   7 -> 3 has to scale the output by 3/7 = 0.43. Every non zero slot instead
#   moved by exactly 0.930: 4260 -> 3960, 4686 -> 4356, 4899 -> 4554,
#   5041 -> 4686. So the weights are in the arithmetic but the output is not
#   weight times activation.
#
#   THE PERMUTATION HALF WAS UNREADABLE, AND THAT WAS THIS FILE'S FAULT. Only
#   4 of the 16 slots moved when the activation was permuted; the other 12 were
#   byte identical. The tool's filter excluded nan, inf and zero and called
#   what was left "finite varying channels", so four constants went into both
#   fits and poisoned them (3873 against 3999, neither readable). WHETHER A
#   SLOT VARIES CAN ONLY BE SEEN ACROSS RUNS. A single run cannot say it, and
#   this is the third round lost to a variant of that sentence.
#
# WHAT THE FOUR SLOT PERIOD MEANS. Read as halves the outputs went in a period
# of four: %4==0 moved with the activation, %4==1 was always 0xffff, %4==2
# moved with the nibble only, %4==3 was always 0x0000. Pair them little endian:
#
#   [94 f1][ff ff] -> 0xfffff194 = -3692
#   [a4 10][00 00] -> 0x000010a4 = +4260
#
# 0xffff and 0x0000 are the high halves of a negative and a positive 32 bit
# integer. Sixteen halves are eight integers of alternating sign, and the four
# slot period is an artefact of reading a 4 byte element 2 bytes at a time.
#
# THIS CORRECTS ROUND 181. Registers 0x40b0 = 1 and 0x40b4 = 0 say the stage
# does not REQUANTISE. "So the output is a float" was added on top of that and
# never measured. An un-requantised output is the raw accumulator, which is an
# integer. The int32 reading is now printed beside the half reading in every
# --f16 run so that whichever is wrong can be seen to be wrong.
#
# ROUND 185'S QUESTION. Stop assuming the width and read it the way the weight
# layout was read, with a sparse probe. --osweep puts ONE live nibble in the
# entire weight buffer, in ONE channel, holds the activation at a single
# constant so nothing in the output can track an activation index, and dumps
# the output bytes with no assumption at all. Then it sweeps the channel.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   one run of bytes moves and its start tracks the channel
#             the run length is the element width and the difference between
#             starts is the channel stride. Both read, neither fitted.
#
#   the same bytes move whatever the channel is
#             the output is not written per channel, and every per channel
#             reading in this tree is wrong, not just the width.
#
#   nothing non zero for any channel
#             one nibble against a constant 8.0 is below whatever the stage
#             keeps. The probe needs a bigger weight, not a new theory.
#
# THE CONTROL THAT CAN FAIL is the first row of the sweep, with NO live nibble
# anywhere. Round 184 measured that as all zero. Anything non zero there means
# the output does not come from the weight buffer and the sweep is unreadable.
#
# AND THE ORDER CHANGED BACK. Round 184 moved the int8 regression to AFTER five
# w4a16 runs and every int8 job then timed out, output left holding the 0xa5
# sentinel, with rk_iommu "Error during raw reset" beside it. The register
# stream is not the cause: int8's stream at 1 512 1024 and at 1 64 64 is BYTE
# IDENTICAL before and after the w4a16 port (checked offline against e46a56d,
# no board needed). Mesa's own regressions ran fine afterwards, so nothing is
# permanently broken. That leaves the order itself, so int8 goes FIRST again
# here. If it passes first and fails last, a w4a16 job leaves the NPU unable to
# start the next one, which is a defect in the stream this project just wrote.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-15 round 184. Every w4a16 reading since round 181 rests on a
# confound, and this round does nothing but break it.
#
# THE CONFOUND. The --f16 probe feeds an activation of (c mod 16) - 16 and puts
# one live nibble per channel at k = c mod 16, and that nibble has been a
# constant 7 in every run from 181 on. So the activation VALUE is a linear
# function of the CHANNEL INDEX, and the two cannot be told apart by anything
# the probe prints. Round 181's fit
#
#   npu = 319 * act - 2412
#
# rewrites as 319 * (c mod 16) + a constant without changing one measured
# number. Worse, the weight has never varied, so no run has ever shown that the
# output depends on the WEIGHTS at all. "The MAC is live, only the coefficient
# path is dead", which is what rounds 182 and 183 were built on, is not
# supported by any data this probe has produced.
#
# TWO KNOBS, and each one has a branch that kills the reading.
#
#   CHARSIU_INT4_PERM=1 permutes which k holds which activation value. The
#     sixteen values, the range and the packing are all unchanged; only the
#     pairing moves, so it is one variable. The tool then fits the outputs
#     against the activation AND against the channel index and prints both,
#     with the RMS residual of each. The residual decides it, not the slope.
#
#   CHARSIU_INT4_LIVE=3 changes the live nibble from 7 to 3. A real MAC has to
#     scale with it. The activation slope has to fall from about 7 to about 3.
#
# THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN.
#
#   activation residual small, channel residual large, and the slope falls
#   7 -> 3 with the nibble
#             the MAC is genuinely live. Rounds 181 to 183 stand and the open
#             question stays the coefficient fetch.
#
#   channel residual small and activation residual large
#             the output is a function of the channel index and NOT of the
#             input. Nothing is being computed on this path; rounds 181 to 183
#             are read wrong and the w4a16 port has to be re-argued from the
#             registers, not from the outputs.
#
#   both residuals large, or fewer than 8 distinct values over the 16 channels
#             the run says nothing and the tool prints TOO FLAT TO JUDGE A FIT.
#             This is the branch round 169 needed and did not have.
#
#   the slope does not move when the nibble goes 7 -> 3
#             the weights are not in the arithmetic, whatever the activation
#             does. This one fires INDEPENDENTLY of the permutation result, and
#             it is the stronger of the two.
#
# ENTRY 3 is the baseline, plain ramp and nibble 7, and it must reproduce round
# 183's numbers. If it does not, the image is not the same experiment and
# nothing below it can be read.
#
# ENTRY 6 is the int8 regression. The int4 branch of the tool moved and the
# shared packer did not, so 1 512 1024 and the 64 wide probes must still come
# back exactly as before. A tool change that quietly broke the known good path
# would make every int4 number here unreadable.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-15 round 183. The coefficient surface is not being FETCHED, and the
# vendor keeps that configuration in its own stream too.
#
# ROUND 182's RESULT, and both branches of its rule fired at once. Four runs,
# CHARSIU_NO_LIFT, CHARSIU_COEF_C=1, the baseline and both knobs together, came
# back BYTE IDENTICAL. The rule said an intercept that does not move means A is
# not reaching the output, and a slope that does not move means C is not read.
# Both, which is the same sentence twice:
#
#   the coefficient buffer charsiu builds is not read at all on the w4a16 path
#
# The MAC is live, since the output still varies with the activation. Only the
# coefficient path is dead.
#
# WHY, and it is the same shape of answer as round 181. The vendor keeps its
# DPU_RDMA configuration in a separate stream as well: none of its 3328 int4
# convolution streams carries a single RDMA register, and the 21 register block
# that does is elsewhere in the same file. Against what charsiu emitted, exactly
# four differ and not one of them is geometry:
#
#   0x501c   00000000 where int8 writes 00000710
#   0x5034   4000004c where int8 writes 00000041
#   0x5040   the ROW COUNT. Another vendor stream carries 0x20 beside a 0x1f in
#            0x500c, so it is 0x500c + 1, and charsiu wrote 0
#   0x5044   000280a1 where int8 writes 40000010
#
# So the DPU stage was ported in round 181 and the unit that FETCHES the surface
# for it was not. That is the whole of why A and C are inert.
#
# ROUND 183's QUESTION, and its decision rule, written before the run.
#
#   THE CONTROL IS THE BEST ONE THIS PROJECT HAS HAD, because round 182 measured
#   it as dead. CHARSIU_NO_LIFT and CHARSIU_COEF_C changed nothing, four runs
#   byte identical. If the surface is fetched now, THEY MUST STOP BEING INERT.
#
#     both knobs move the output
#               the surface is fetched, the coefficient path is live, and the
#               slope and the intercept can be chased where round 182 could not
#               touch them.
#     neither moves
#               the four registers are not what fetches it, and the next place
#               is the record format rather than the configuration.
#     the output stops varying with the activation
#               something in these four broke the MAC as well, and they go back
#               one at a time rather than as a unit.
#
#   ENTRY 3 is the baseline, ENTRY 4 and ENTRY 5 the two knobs that were dead.
#   ENTRY 6 IS THE int8 CONTROL: all four values are inside an int4 branch and
#   int8 emitted byte for byte what it did before, checked on the desktop.
#
