# 2026-08-15 round 182. The datapath is LIVE. Two constants are wrong and each
# has a named suspect, so each gets one entry.
#
# ROUND 181's RESULT. With the vendor's w4a16 output stage ported, the output
# VARIES WITH THE ACTIVATION for the first time in the whole int4 arc. Every
# round before this one came back flat against the input.
#
#   c    act    npu (half)
#   0    -16    -11424
#   4    -12    -10856
#   8     -8    -10288
#   12    -4     -9720
#
# Differences of +568, +568, +568, so a clean straight line: slope 142 per unit
# of activation and intercept -9152, where the expected line is 7 * act + 0. So
# the SHAPE is right and two constants are wrong, the slope by 20.3 times and an
# intercept that should not be there at all.
#
# The other three channels in four came back -nan, about 0.001, and exactly zero.
# The period is 4, while the impulse's own period is 16, so the channel to
# coefficient mapping is not what it was under the integer stage either. That is
# left alone this round: it needs the w4a16 coefficient RECORD, which is data
# rather than registers and cannot be read out of a .rkllm.
#
# WHY THIS ROUND IS TWO KNOBS RATHER THAN A THEORY. Both constants have a named
# suspect and both suspects are already reachable.
#
#   the intercept   THE LIFT. It exists to clear an integer floor, and w4a16
#                   requantises with 0x40b0 = 1 and 0x40b4 = 0, an identity, so
#                   there is no floor and A lands in the output raw.
#   the slope       THE C RECORD, which this tree writes as 16, Q4 for a relative
#                   scale of one. That is the int8 convention and nothing says it
#                   means the same here. The whole coefficient buffer is still
#                   built to int8 conventions; only the DPU stage was ported.
#
# ROUND 182's QUESTION, and its decision rule, written before the run. The probe
# now prints a least squares FIT over the channels that come back finite and
# varying, because round 181 was read by eyeballing four numbers and computing a
# slope by hand, which is how a sweep stride got read as a row size in round 173.
#
#   ENTRY 3, CHARSIU_NO_LIFT=1.
#     the intercept goes to about zero    the lift is the intercept, and it has
#                                         no business on a float path.
#     the intercept does not move         something else adds it, and A is not
#                                         reaching the output the way this
#                                         assumes.
#
#   ENTRY 4, CHARSIU_COEF_C=1 against the default 16.
#     the slope moves by about 16         C is the gain, and the remaining
#                                         factor is then the fp16 scale table.
#     the slope does not move             C is not read on this path at all, and
#                                         the gain is in the scale table or the
#                                         weight nibble's own interpretation.
#
#   ENTRY 5 is round 181's baseline unchanged, so the two knobs are read against
#   the same run rather than against a remembered one.
#
#   ENTRY 6 IS THE CONTROL and int8 must still be byte exact.
