# ROUND 178's QUESTION, and its decision rule, written before the run.
#
#   ENTRY 3, --kpair. ONE live nibble in the whole buffer, a ONE HOT input at a
#   single k, sweeping k across all of K. The output is nonzero exactly when that
#   nibble is paired with that k, so the set of inputs a nibble touches is read
#   directly with no sum to unpick. Eight byte offsets, both nibbles.
#
#     exactly one k lights per nibble
#               the pairing is one to one, the layout is a permutation after
#               all, and the k column can finally be built from these hits
#               rather than from a value.
#     several k light per nibble
#               a nibble is broadcast across inputs and the count says by how
#               much. That would explain the map's unusable k column AND round
#               177's saturation with one mechanism, and it would mean the
#               weight buffer is not a plain tiling at this precision.
#     no k lights at an offset the map says is live
#               the one hot input is below what the output stage can show, and
#               the amplitude has to come up before anything else is read.
#
#   ENTRY 4 keeps round 177's impulse as the baseline, so the saturation this
#   round is explaining stays on the same page as the explanation.
