#   int4 impulse     54 of 64 exact, the other 10 off by 1 or 2
#   int4 dense 64    6 of 64
#   int4 dense 512   95 of 1024
#
# The first reading of that was "the layout is right and the accumulation is
# not". THAT READING IS WITHDRAWN. The weight scale was left at int8's while a
# nibble spans -7 to 7 instead of -128 to 127, so the whole reference vector fell
# into -1 to +1. Of the impulse's 54 matches, essentially all are channels where
# the reference is zero and the hardware is zero, which is not a match at all;
# and every channel that carried any signal was wrong by about half of the range
# it could hold. An error of one output count is 1250 accumulator units here, and
# a single live tap can only produce 120 of them.
#
# So the only clean result in round 169 is the bias ramp, and the bias ramp does
# not touch a weight: it holds the MAC at zero and measures the output stage
# alone. int4's registers, requant, lift and offset are right. Its LAYOUT IS NOT
# CONFIRMED, and neither is anything else about its weight path.
#
# A matching channel is not a computed one. This project has a rule about that,
# from the Mesa side, and round 169 broke it.
#
# ROUND 170's QUESTION, and its decision rule, written before the run.
#
#   The int4 weight scale is 18 times int8's now, which puts the accumulator
#   range where int8's is, so a match means something and one count means one
#   count. Every result line also prints the REFERENCE's own spread beside the
#   match count, and says TOO FLAT TO JUDGE A MATCH when it has fewer than eight
#   distinct values, so round 169's mistake cannot be made quietly again. The
#   impulse uses a real nibble of 7 rather than an int8 constant masked down to 4.
