# it.
#
# 2026-08-15 round 167. int4, because it is the only 2x left. The registers for
# it are confirmed offline; the LAYOUT is what the board has to say.
#
# ROUND 166 DID NOT BREAK THE READING. Seven shapes at 32 chained tasks, and the
# two pairs put in to break it held:
#
#   1.05 MB as K=1024 N=1024 and as K=2048 N=512   111.56 and 111.24 us, 0.3% apart
#   2.10 MB as K=2048 N=1024 and as K=1024 N=2048  208.06 and 211.21 us, 1.5% apart
#
# Fitting all seven together gives us per task = 26.3 + weight_MB * 84.3, which is
# 11.9 GB/s plus 26 us, every point inside 10% of the line and most inside 4%. A
# further 172 us of fixed cost sits on each submit and is what chaining removes.
# The CPU denominator works now and reads 5078 us against the NPU's 201.
#
# So decode is DRAM bound. Llama-3.2-1B reads about 973 M projection weights per
# token, once each: int8 is 85 ms and 11.8 tokens a second, int4 is 44 ms and
# 22.7, against the vendor's shipped 13. int4 is the only 2x on the table.
#
# WHAT IS ALREADY SETTLED ABOUT int4, offline, from the vendor's own file. The
# .rkllm carries 3328 four bit streams and 40 eight bit ones, so the precisions
# can be diffed against each other with no board at all
# (vendor-capture/int4_regs.py):
#
#   0x100c   0x00000000 int8, 0x00600120 int4, plus 0x20000000 for 16 bit
#            activations
#   0x101c   real bytes, k * n / 2
#   0x1020   k / 2, and 0x1030's high half the same, where int8 carries twice
#   surf     follows the ACTIVATION precision and not the weight precision
#
# All of those were already right in charsiu EXCEPT 0x100c, which its job emitter
# wrote as 0 for int4 as well as int8, so an int4 job would have asked for an
# int8 weight fetch and any layout read from it would have been noise. Its other
# emitter had the right constant. Fixed this round.
#
# ROUND 167's QUESTION, and its decision rule, written before the run. What no
# file contains is the LAYOUT: a .rkllm gives geometry, not the order the nibbles
# sit in, and the group of 64 in charsiu is copied from the RK3588 notes and has
# never been confirmed here. The question is which two weights share a byte:
#
#   K PAIRING   [n/32][k/64][n%32][k%64], the low nibble is an even k
#   N PAIRING   [n/64][k/32][n%64][k%32], the low nibble is an even n
#
#   ENTRY 3 fills every low nibble live and every high nibble dead, and the two
#   answer differently in a way no scale or requant can disguise, because the
#   signal is WHICH CHANNELS ARE NONZERO rather than what value they hold.
#
#     every channel nonzero and roughly equal
#               nibbles pair along K. Half of each channel's taps are live.
#     even and odd channels split, half live and half dead
#               nibbles pair along N.
#     something else
#               neither hypothesis, and the first byte entry says where the
#               origin is before guessing again.
#
#   THE FIRST TWO LINES ARE THE CONTROL and must be read first. Every nibble
#   dead must come back flat; every nibble live must not. The vendor only ever
#   runs int4 weights with 16 bit activations and charsiu has never run a 16 bit
#   activation at all, so this asks for int4 weights with the int8 activations
#   that are known to work.
#
#     dead and live look the same
#               that combination does not compute, no layout can be read from
#               the rest of the entry, and the next round is fp16 activations
#               rather than another layout guess.
#
