# 2026-08-15 round 165. The matmul is correct. The question is now whether it is
# worth computing here at all, which is a RATIO and not a number.
#
# ROUND 164 LANDED. Every value identical to the CPU reference rather than close
# to it, and the control brought the fault straight back:
#
#   dense          M=1 K=64  N=64      64 of 64 bytes exact
#   negative MAC   M=1 K=64  N=64      64 of 64, output -66 to +63
#   bias ramp      M=1 K=64  N=64      64 of 64
#   impulse        M=1 K=64  N=64      64 of 64
#   dense          M=1 K=512 N=1024    1024 of 1024, fit slope 1.0000
#   CHARSIU_NO_LIFT control            0 of 64, floor back at the rail
#
# So charsiu computes a signed int8 matmul end to end. Both of the bugs behind
# the last twelve rounds were things this tree had written down backwards: the
# input needed the same -0x80 bias the weight had, and the output stage is
# clamp(max(requant, 0) + offset, -128, 127), an int8 with a real fused ReLU
# under it that stops mattering once the accumulator is lifted by 128 and the
# offset takes the same 128 back.
#
# ROUND 165's QUESTION. A projection at M = 1, K = 2048, N = 1024 is about 4 MOP.
# At this hardware's rated 6 TOPS that is UNDER A MICROSECOND of arithmetic, so
# anything measurable around it is dispatch and not MAC. The RK3588 open stacks
# measured that and concluded a single row matmul belongs on the CPU; the RK3576
# vendor dispatches 3752 of them in its own Llama file and ships about 13 tokens
# a second. Both cannot be right for the same shape.
#
# /opt/charsiu/charsiu_bench sweeps tasks per job from 1 to 128 and prints
# microseconds per submit, per matmul, and GOP/s, then two jobs of eight to see
# whether the scheduler uses the second core, then the same matmul on one CPU
# thread as the denominator. Tasks inside a job are chained on one core with no
# further ioctl; jobs are what can be spread.
#
#   DECISION RULE, written before the run.
#
#     us/matmul FALLS as tasks per job rises
#               the floor is the submit and the fence, chaining gets under it,
#               and a runtime batches a token's projections into one submit.
#               The number to carry is the one at 32 or 64 tasks.
#     us/matmul is FLAT
#               the cost is per task and batching cannot help. Then the CPU line
#               decides whether M = 1 belongs on this hardware at all, and if it
#               does not, the RK3588 conclusion holds here too and the runtime
#               is built around prefill rather than decode.
#     two jobs of eight beats one job of sixteen
#               the second core is reachable from one process, which doubles
#               whatever the answer above turns out to be.
#
#   ENTRY 2 IS THE CONTROL, and it is not about speed. charsiu_submit was
#   rewritten this round to take a list of jobs and tasks, with the old single
#   task call becoming a wrapper over it. The 512 to 1024 matmul MUST still come
#   back 1024 of 1024 BYTE EXACT. If the refactor moved a byte, no timing taken
#   through it means anything.
#
